    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34636": {
      "title": "Division Chief, Division of General Internal Medicine and Hospital Medicine",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Division Chief, Division of General Internal Medicine and Hospital Medicine Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Division Chief, Division of General Internal Medicine and Hospital Medicine Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application Process Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae and a letter of interest describing their vision for academic general internal medicine, primary care, and hospital medicine, and their leadership philosophy. Finalists will present a seminar on their assessment of the way forward for academic general internal medicine and will participate in panel conversations with faculty and staff from across the division. Posting Details Position Details Title Division Chief, Division of General Internal Medicine and Hospital Medicine Specific Title Associate/ Professor of Medicine Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Medicine - General Internal Medicine Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Opportunity The Department of Medicine at Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) seeks an accomplished academic physician leader to serve as the founding Chief of a new Division of General Internal Medicine and Hospital Medicine. Two new divisions are being established through the strategic restructuring of the current Division of General Internal Medicine and Geriatrics, the second being the new ‘Division of Aging, Geriatric Medicine, and Palliative Care’. This restructuring is a deliberate investment in focused leadership intended to sustain and accelerate academic growth and excellence in the Department’s generalist and acute care missions. The founding Chief will lead approximately 280 faculty members organized into three sections, each with its own strategic plan and identity, and will report directly to the Chair of the Department of Medicine. This is a rare opportunity to define, from the outset, the structure, culture, and strategic direction of one of the largest general medicine and hospital medicine enterprises in American academic medicine. The Division: An Overview of Sections and Research Centers Division faculty have the opportunity to engage in the service, research, and education missions of the Department and IUSM . Faculty practice and teach across the full continuum of undergraduate and graduate medical education and hold impactful Departmental and School educational leadership roles, including the internal medicine residency program directorship, multiple Key Clinician Educator appointments, and clerkship and course leadership positions. Research faculty maintain deep partnerships with various Research Centers including the Center for Biomedical Informatics and Health Services Research at the Regenstrief Institute, the Center for Health Communication and Information at the Richard L. Roudebush VA Medical Center, the IU Center for Biomedical Ethics, the Center for Global Health, and the Regenstrief Center for Health Equity Research at Eskenazi Health. Researchers have consistently secured close to $3M dollars in federal and non-federal funding annually over the last three years. Examples of current research include diabetes prevention, improving the care of individuals with rare diseases, implementation of survivorship care plans, and the deployment of large-scale decision aids to guide colorectal cancer screening. • Section of Hospital Medicine (approximately 180 faculty members). The largest section in the Department, with faculty engaged in clinical care, scholarship and research, and teaching in hospital medicine care across IU Health, Eskenazi Health, and the Richard L. Roudebush VA Medical Center. • Section of General Internal Medicine (approximately 15 faculty members). The academic home for general internal medicine, including research faculty in health services research, biomedical informatics, global health, health equity, and medical ethics, and faculty who hold key educational leadership roles across the Department of Medicine and IUSM . • Section of Primary Care Internal Medicine (approximately 85 faculty members). Ambulatory internal medicine practice and general internal medicine care is an area of critical workforce need statewide and a stated development priority of the division’s faculty. Faculty provide general internal medicine and medicine-pediatrics care across IU Health, Eskenazi Health, and the Richard L Roudebush VA Medical Center. A retrospective of the Division’s activities in the academic year 2025-26 can be viewed here . Position Summary Reporting to the Chair of the Department of Medicine, the Division Chief provides academic, clinical, financial, and operational leadership for the division across all sites and sections. The Chief recruits, develops, and retains faculty; sets and executes the Division’s strategic plan in alignment with both the Department of Medicine 2026 to 2031 Strategic Plan and IUSM’s strategic priorities ; oversees the division’s budget and clinical performance in partnership with our health system partners; advances the Division’s research, scholarship, and educational missions; and represents the Division within the Department, the School, our affiliated health systems, and nationally. This leadership role is expected to reflect a mix of clinical practice and research or scholarly activity, configured with the Chair to fit the successful candidate’s academic profile. Key Responsibilities Vision and strategy • Develop, in partnership with faculty, staff, and the Chair, the founding strategic plan for the Division and for each of its three sections. • Build a Division leadership team with clear accountability and create visible leadership development opportunities for faculty across all three sections. • Align the Division’s missions across sites, employer groups, and stakeholders, and represent the Division’s interests in health system planning. Education • Elevate the Division’s undergraduate and graduate medical education mission, in close partnership with the internal medicine residency, the medical student clerkship, and IUSM educational leadership. • Support the academic advancement of clinician educators, including scholarship in teaching and education, and build the infrastructure that makes educational scholarship sustainable. Clinical care and primary care growth • Elevate academic internal medicine primary care, including recruitment, panel growth, practice model innovation, and the visibility of primary care within the Division and the health system. • Partner with IU Health, Eskenazi Health, and the VA on hospital medicine operations, quality and safety, throughput, care transitions, and value-based care. • Evaluate clinical workloads and targets and work with clinical partners to align them with academic success and clinician wellbeing. Research and scholarship • Sustain and grow research and scholarship infrastructure across education, general medicine, and hospital medicine, including strategic recruitment. • Foster scholarship collaborations across sections, Divisions, and Departments, supporting the Departmental goal of advancing IUSM’s national NIH funding position. Faculty development and culture • Create dedicated pathways and supportive infrastructure for faculty advancement in teaching, clinical excellence, leadership, quality improvement, scholarship, and research at every site. • Advance clinician wellbeing as an explicit division priority. • Develop a centralized, equitable process for academic recruitment across sites. • Retain and grow collegiality, community, and connection among sections, divisions, and the school. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Vibrant City, Exceptional Suburbs Indianapolis offers a thriving downtown and family-friendly suburbs. Many faculty choose to live in Carmel, Zionsville, and Fishers, which are frequently ranked among the best places to live nationally. The region combines top-rated public and private schools, affordable housing, and cultural amenities with a strong life sciences economy. For additional information on life in Indy, please visit click here . Comprehensive benefits package through Indiana University, one of the state’s largest employers. Dual-employment offers unique investment opportunities (401(k) plus 457(b) and 403(b), dual Roth contributions, HSA rollover). Tuition benefit for dependent students attending Indiana University. Relocation assistance and dual-career support. The Institution and Research Centers Indiana University School of Medicine is the largest medical school in the United States, with campuses distributed across Indiana. The Department of Medicine is among its largest departments and delivers care and education in partnership with IU Health, Eskenazi Health, and the Richard L. Roudebush VA Medical Center, with a longstanding research partnership with the Regenstrief Institute. Indianapolis offers a substantial metropolitan setting with a low cost of living, an established cultural and culinary scene, and easy access to the region. Indiana University Health represents the largest network of clinicians in the state of Indiana with facilities across the state. Eskenazi health serves as the public hospital division of the Health & Hospital Corporation of Marion County and the VA serves our country’s vets. Spread across seven sites, hospitalist clinicians in our Division provide care to nearly a quarter million patients annually. Hospital medicine anchors the educational experience for our internal medicine inpatient rotations for medical students and residents. Hospital medicine faculty also serve as leaders in hospital operations, quality, and process improvement efforts. Primary care similarly spans the state through our health partners and includes medicine-pediatrics trained clinicians. All Eskenazi Health primary care locations are designated Federally Qualified Health Centers ( FQHC ) and serve as a critical healthcare safety net and ensure access to affordable, comprehensive healthcare for underserved and vulnerable populations. The Roudebush Indianapolis downtown VA provides primary care to nearly 24,000 veterans. Our primary care sites also serve as the educational and training anchor for general medicine outpatient rotations. Research centers affiliated with this Division currently include the Center for Health Services Research , the Center for Biomedical Informatics , the Center for Global Health , the Center for Bioethics , the Regenstrief Center for Health Equity Research at Eskenazi Health, and the Center for Health Information and Communication. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications • Terminal degree or doctorate degree required, with board certification and continuous certification in internal medicine. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Indiana. • Credentials commensurate with appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor at Indiana University School of Medicine. Appointment may be on the tenure track or on a non-tenure track; tenure track eligibility is welcomed but not required. • A record of clinical activity in general internal medicine, primary care internal medicine, or hospital medicine sufficient to sustain credibility with and insight into the working lives of clinicians and clinician educators. • Progressive and successful operational leadership within a large, complex academic or health system environment, including demonstrable success developing and deploying a program across multiple sites and stakeholder groups and leading people through organizational change. • A record of scholarship, which may be in education, general internal medicine, hospital medicine, health services research, or a related domain, sufficient to command the respect of a large and academically varied faculty. • Demonstrated success in mentorship and in faculty or staff development. Department Contact for Questions Stephanie Wallace, Associate Director of Academic Administration slw@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications • Leadership or sustained participation in formal faculty development programming. • Regional or national leadership in societies relevant to general internal medicine, primary care, or hospital medicine, and/or editorial or peer review leadership in relevant journals. • Extramural funding in undergraduate or graduate medical education, general internal medicine, hospital medicine, or health services research. • Completion of formal training, coursework, or degrees that strengthen financial, communication, leadership, mentorship, or management skills, reflecting a personal commitment to lifelong learning. • Experience leading in a multi-site environment that includes academic, safety net, and Veterans Affairs settings. Attributes We Value Division faculty and staff were surveyed during the design of this position. They asked for a leader characterized by empathy, genuine listening, a collaborative approach, respect, problem solving skills, flexibility, and approachability, and for communication and presentation skills that both inspire the team and enable effective advocacy on its behalf. Special Instructions Application Process Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae and a letter of interest describing their vision for academic general internal medicine, primary care, and hospital medicine, and their leadership philosophy. Finalists will present a seminar on their assessment of the way forward for academic general internal medicine and will participate in panel conversations with faculty and staff from across the division. Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02531-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34634": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Specific Title Neonatologist Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Division of Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking to add a clinical track faculty physician to provide patient care and serve as a clinician educator. Expert neonatologists at Riley Children’s Health deliver specialized, evidence-based care to neonates in need of lifesaving choices. In many cases, our care begins prenatally and continues through the transition to home with comprehensive, multidisciplinary follow-up. Clinically, this role is expected to perform approximately fourteen (14) weeks of clinical service on the Riley Neonatal Progressive Care Unit, twenty-four (24) weekend rounding shifts covering inpatient service on the Riley Neonatal Progressive Care Unit with at least one (1) minor and one (1) major holiday annually When not on inpatient service or vacation or conference, the expectation is to cover two (2) full day outpatient clinics in the Early Years Program. As an educator, there are numerous activities to advance programs within the department at both the student and resident level across all campuses. Faculty are involved in supervision and instruction of residents and medical students on clinical services. We also encourage participation in various didactic programs, as well as activities in the skills lab and simulation center. There are numerous activities to advance our research programs within the department and we anticipate this individual to actively participate and facilitate research activities either through direct investigator lead research projects or via referral of patients to ongoing clinical research projects. The Department of Pediatrics at IUSM /Riley Hospital for Children is a 398-member faculty that is consistently within the top 10 pediatric departments in NIH funded research in the United States and has 19 Fellowship programs. There is a new large pediatric clinical research center, a new pediatric outpatient center dedicated to general pediatrics and neurodevelopment related divisions in pediatrics, genetics, and psychiatry. There are also State-of-the-art laboratories in a dedicated pediatric research building, a clinical research center, a well-functioning biobank, six T32 fellow training grants and one K12s that support research in the Department. Our nationally recognized Division of Children’s Health Research is available for collaboration and as a resource for combined fellowships with the pediatric hospitalist program. The Department also has research relationships with the Regenstrief Institute in Health Informatics. Pediatric Radiology and Pediatric Neuroradiology are strong research partners. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree required. Completion of pediatric residency and Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine fellowship programs. Must be Board Certified/Eligible in Pediatrics & Neonatology. New graduates and experienced providers are encouraged to apply. Department Contact for Questions Jacqueline Boese jhboese@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02535-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? (Open Ended Question) * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34633": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Specific Title Neonatologist Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Division of Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking to add a clinical track faculty physician to provide patient care and serve as a clinician educator. Expert neonatologists at Riley Children’s Health deliver specialized, evidence-based care to neonates in need of lifesaving choices. In many cases, our care begins prenatally and continues through the transition to home with comprehensive, multidisciplinary follow-up. Clinically, this role is expected to perform a total of twenty (20) weeks of clinical service at Riley Hospital NICUs, including Van Dyke NICU , STORK Team, and the Heart Center NICU . On an annual basis, your call responsibilities will consist of twenty-four (24) in-house calls, eight (8) weekend rounding shifts, and twelve (12) additional in-house moonlighting calls paid at $175/hr. Additional moonlighting will be available, and shifts may be picked up at your discretion. As an educator, there are numerous activities to advance programs within the department at both the student and resident level across all campuses. Faculty are involved in supervision and instruction of residents and medical students on clinical services. We also encourage participation in various didactic programs, as well as activities in the skills lab and simulation center. There are numerous activities to advance our research programs within the department and we anticipate this individual to actively participate and facilitate research activities either through direct investigator lead research projects or via referral of patients to ongoing clinical research projects. The Department of Pediatrics at IUSM /Riley Hospital for Children is a 398-member faculty that is consistently within the top 10 pediatric departments in NIH funded research in the United States and has 19 Fellowship programs. There is a new large pediatric clinical research center, a new pediatric outpatient center dedicated to general pediatrics and neurodevelopment related divisions in pediatrics, genetics, and psychiatry. There are also State-of-the-art laboratories in a dedicated pediatric research building, a clinical research center, a well-functioning biobank, six T32 fellow training grants and one K12s that support research in the Department. Our nationally recognized Division of Children’s Health Research is available for collaboration and as a resource for combined fellowships with the pediatric hospitalist program. The Department also has research relationships with the Regenstrief Institute in Health Informatics. Pediatric Radiology and Pediatric Neuroradiology are strong research partners. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree required. Completion of pediatric residency and Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine fellowship programs. Must be Board Certified/Eligible in Pediatrics & Neonatology. New graduates and experienced providers are encouraged to apply. Department Contact for Questions Jacqueline Boese jhboese@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02536-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? (Open Ended Question) * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34628": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Department of Kinesiology – School of Health & Human Sciences Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Kinesiology in the School of Health & Human Sciences ( SHHS ) at Indiana University Indianapolis invites applications for a full-time, 10-month, tenure track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. The successful applicant will have the ability to teach and mentor both graduate (MS and PhD) and undergraduate (BS) students, will be responsible for developing an active and sustainable research agenda in their area of expertise, generate external funding, engage in teaching, and provide service to the school, university, community, and profession. Individuals with translational research and/or community engagement experiences are especially encouraged to apply. The Department of Kinesiology offers a BS with plans of study in: - Exercise Science - Applied Fitness and Sports Performance - Physical Education Teacher Education - Nutrition and Wellness SHHS also offers PhD and MS degrees in Human Performance and Rehabilitation Sciences. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 1, 2026, and will be accepted until the position is filled. The target starting date is August 1, 2027. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their letter of application, curriculum vitae, statements of research and teaching, and the names and contact information for three references. The letter of intent should include a summary of qualifications and experience, key motivation for application, and alignment with IU Indianapolis and SHHS , overall. IU Indianapolis is Indiana’s premier urban public research university and holds Community Engagement and R1 research Carnegie Classifications. It is home to more than 22,000 students pursuing degrees across 15 distinct schools, including the Indiana University School of Medicine and the Indiana Center for Musculoskeletal Health ( ICMH ). IU Indianapolis has earned national recognition for its innovative health and life sciences initiatives, providing extensive opportunities for collaborative, interdisciplinary research. The university is making significant investments in research, including the launch of two new institutes: the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) and the Institute for Human Health and Wellbeing (H2W). Additionally, a $138 million grant from the Lilly Endowment will support the IU LAB , a state-of-the-art facility at 16 Tech site designed to position Indiana as a national leader in bioscience discovery, commercialization, and talent development. SHHS is looking for scholars who will contribute to our commitment to engage the city, state, nation, and beyond through research, teaching, and service. Potential scholars should take into consideration and value the attributes of our campus and city populations as we prepare our graduates to enter and lead in an increasingly complex world. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . Individuals who require a reasonable accommodation in order to participate in the search process must notify the School’s HR Business Partner, Myrna Palacios, at mypala@iu.edu , in advance. Basic Qualifications Ph.D. in Exercise Science, Kinesiology, Human Movement Science, or closely related field. Demonstrated teaching and/or professional experience in: Sport and Human Performance Testing and Interpretation. Exercise Leadership and Program Design. Behavioral Aspects of Physical Activity and Exercise. Active research agenda aligned with the discipline of kinesiology. Demonstrated potential or history of securing independent external research funding. Experience in mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and/or professional students. Department Contact for Questions For more information on the position, please contact department chair Dr. Mark Urtel at murtel1@iu.edu. Additional information on the School of Health & Human Sciences, as well as the Department of Kinesiology is available at: https://shhs.indianapolis.iu.edu/index.html . Additional Qualifications Post-doctoral training or equivalent translational field/industry experience. Active interest or demonstrated success in interdisciplinary research collaborations across SHHS , the IU School of Medicine, or campus research institutes (e.g., ICMH , CBATI , H2W). A demonstrated ability to communicate and collaborate effectively with students, employees, and other members of the university community. Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101716-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34627": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Open Rank) in Nursing",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Lecturer (Open Rank) in Nursing Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Lecturer (Open Rank) in Nursing Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit an online application, cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, peer review of teaching, and list of professional references (3 minimum) to be considered for this position. Posting Details Position Details Title Lecturer (Open Rank) in Nursing Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Columbus - Behavioral, Health and Life Sciences Location Columbus Position Summary Indiana University Columbus is committed to fostering a welcoming campus community where everyone feels valued and empowered to contribute their unique perspectives that informs academic excellence. We desire candidates whose work contributes to the three pillars established by Indiana University’s Student Success and Opportunity, Transformative Research and Creativity, and Service to our State and Beyond. IU Columbus strives to be the destination for those who seek a high-quality education and a welcoming, distinctive small university experience that prepares our students to collaborate and contribute in meaningful and significant ways both locally and globally. Our mission is to advance the state of Indiana and the intellectual growth of its citizens to the highest levels nationally and internationally through research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and community engagement by offering a distinctive blend of bachelor’s, master’s, and professional degrees. The IU Columbus School of Nursing in the Division of Behavioral, Health, and Life Sciences invites applications for a full-time, open-rank Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, or Teaching Professor in Nursing. This is a 10-month, non-tenure track faculty appointment with an anticipated start date of January 2027. Lecturer Track Responsibilities The Lecturer rank is responsible for teaching clinical and/or didactic courses in the undergraduate program and other courses as appropriate. Specific functions and assignments are outlined by Department Chairperson, Campus Dean/Director, or Course Leader. Responsibilities include teaching nursing students in accordance with area of graduate preparation and/or professional experience; evaluating students in accordance with the course competencies and requirements; assuming Course Leader responsibilities as needed; participating in university and/or community service in support of teaching; mentoring new faculty members as needed; and participating in professional development. Basic Qualifications REQUIREMENTS : Master’s degree in nursing ( MSN ) and two years of current clinical experience. Candidates must have active Indiana RN license eligibility (or current license). Teaching experience is preferred. Clinical teaching assignments may be in healthcare settings such as area hospitals therefore recent clinical experience in the area of teaching responsibilities is preferred. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding this position can be directed to Kimdy Le, Division Head of Behavioral, Life and Health Sciences, via kimle@iu.edu . Review of applications begins immediately and will continue until all positions are filled and departmental needs are met. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Starting salary range of $62,500 - $65,000 for Lecturer Track Nursing Faculty Special Instructions Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit an online application, cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, peer review of teaching, and list of professional references (3 minimum) to be considered for this position. For Best Consideration Date 10/26/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101720-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34624": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Functional Genomics and Human Disease Mechanisms",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Functional Genomics and Human Disease Mechanisms Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Functional Genomics and Human Disease Mechanisms Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Functional Genomics and Human Disease Mechanisms Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medical & Molecular Genetics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated and creative Postdoctoral Fellow in Functional Genomics and Human Disease Mechanisms to join the Azaiez-Booth Laboratory in Indianapolis, Indiana. Our laboratory uses human genetics and functional genomics to understand how disruption of genes, transcripts, and regulatory elements produces human disease. A central goal of the laboratory is to move beyond genetic association or variant annotation to experimentally determine the molecular and cellular consequences of disease-associated genomic variation. The successful candidate will lead mechanistic studies investigating genes and regulatory loci implicated through human genomic analyses. One major area of investigation involves long noncoding RNAs and regulatory genomic regions disrupted by structural variation, although the position is intentionally broader than lncRNA biology. The fellow will have opportunities to pursue diverse projects in which human genetic discoveries create compelling biological questions that require experimental investigation. This position is particularly well suited for a scientist interested in mechanistic biology, developmental or cellular genetics, and translating human genomic findings into experimentally testable models. Research Opportunities Projects may include: ·         Functional characterization of disease-associated genes, noncoding transcripts, and regulatory elements. ·         Investigation of structural variants that alter gene regulation or disrupt noncoding genomic loci. ·         Development of cellular models for studying candidate disease mechanisms. · CRISPR /Cas-mediated genetic perturbation and genome engineering. ·         Gene-expression and regulatory studies. ·         Transcriptomic and other genomic approaches for defining downstream consequences of genetic perturbation. ·         Mechanistic investigation of genes emerging from human gene-discovery studies. ·         Functional follow-up of candidate disease genes requiring additional experimental evidence. ·         Integration of molecular and cellular findings with human genetic and phenotypic data. A particular strength of this position will be the opportunity to work across both deep mechanistic projects and focused functional studies arising from ongoing gene-discovery efforts. This structure allows the fellow to develop a major hypothesis-driven research direction while also contributing to shorter, high-value projects that can rapidly generate biological insight. This work will include in vivo studies using mouse models along with cell- and molecular-based assays. Prior animal research experience is preferred but not required; however, candidates must be willing to work with mice and to learn the animal-handling and experimental procedures necessary for their projects. Training and Career Development Postdoctoral fellows will receive close mentorship while being encouraged to develop increasing scientific independence. Training will emphasize formulation of research questions, rigorous experimental design, manuscript preparation, scientific presentation, fellowship and grant writing, mentoring of junior researchers, and development of an independent research program. The fellow will have opportunities to collaborate broadly with investigators in human genetics, clinical genetics, genomics, molecular biology, and related disciplines across Indiana University School of Medicine and collaborating institutions. We especially encourage applicants who want to build a research career connecting human genomic discovery with experimental disease biology. Fellows will be supported in developing independent questions and preliminary data that can form the basis for future fellowship, career-development, and independent research applications. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community, and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health and well-being throughout the state of Indiana. Department Contact for Questions Applicants should submit: Curriculum Vitae Letter of application, including brief statement of research interests and career goals Contact information for three references Through the Indiana University academic careers portal via this link https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34621 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Basic Qualifications Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in genetics, molecular biology, developmental biology, cell biology, genomics, neuroscience, biomedical sciences, or a related field by the start of the appointment. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in one or more of the following areas: ·         Functional genetics or functional genomics. ·         Mammalian cell culture. · CRISPR /Cas genome editing. ·         Developmental or cellular disease models. · RNA biology. ·         Gene-expression or transcriptional regulation. ·         Noncoding RNA or regulatory genomics. ·         Structural variation. ·         Transcriptomics or other sequencing-based approaches. ·         Molecular cloning and biochemical assays. Expertise specifically in lncRNA biology is not required. Candidates with a strong background in experimental genetics or molecular and cellular biology who are interested in applying their expertise to human disease mechanisms are strongly encouraged to apply. The ideal candidate will be intellectually curious, experimentally rigorous, and comfortable moving between genetic observations and mechanistic questions. The ability to independently design experiments, interpret complex results, troubleshoot experimental systems, and communicate findings clearly will be important for success. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position and application materials may be directed to: Dr. Hela Azaiez, PhD (hazaiez@iu.edu) and Dr. Kevin T.A. Booth PhD, FACMG (kevbooth@iu.edu). Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02534-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34623": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Auditory Genetics and In Vivo Functional Genomics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Auditory Genetics and In Vivo Functional Genomics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Auditory Genetics and In Vivo Functional Genomics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Auditory Genetics and In Vivo Functional Genomics Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medical & Molecular Genetics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow in Auditory Genetics and In Vivo Functional Genomics to join the Azaiez-Booth Laboratory in Indianapolis, Indiana. Our laboratory investigates the genetic and molecular mechanisms underlying human disease, with a major research program focused on genetic hearing loss and the functional biology of the auditory system. We integrate human genetics with molecular biology, genome editing, functional genomics, and in vivo disease modeling to determine how genetic perturbations alter auditory function and to identify opportunities for therapeutic intervention. The successful candidate will play a central role in expanding the laboratory’s in vivo auditory genetics program. This position is particularly well suited for a scientist interested in connecting human genetic discoveries to organismal phenotypes through mouse models, auditory physiology, molecular analysis, and experimental manipulation of the inner ear. Current and developing projects include functional characterization of genes associated with hearing loss, rapid generation of somatic CRISPR -based models, characterization of genetically engineered disease models, and development of in vivo approaches for manipulating gene activity within the auditory system. These studies provide opportunities to investigate fundamental mechanisms of auditory biology while also developing experimental platforms with translational and therapeutic applications. Research Opportunities The successful candidate will have opportunities to participate in and lead studies involving: ·         Generation and characterization of mouse models of genetic hearing loss. ·         Somatic CRISPR /Cas genome editing and in vivo functional genomics. ·         Auditory phenotyping, including auditory brainstem response and otoacoustic-emission measurements. ·         Inner-ear tissue collection, histology, immunofluorescence, and quantitative imaging. ·         Cochlear and inner-ear experimental approaches. ·         Molecular characterization of genetically perturbed auditory tissues. ·         Investigation of mechanisms connecting genetic perturbation with auditory and vestibular dysfunction. ·         Development and evaluation of therapeutic approaches, including gene-regulatory and gene-based strategies. ·         Integration of experimental findings with human genetic and clinical data. The laboratory is particularly interested in developing rapid and scalable strategies for testing gene function in vivo, allowing human genetic observations to be translated efficiently into experimentally testable hypotheses. Training and Career Development The Azaiez-Booth Laboratory provides a collaborative training environment designed to help postdoctoral fellows develop both technical expertise and scientific independence. Fellows will receive mentorship in experimental design, auditory and genetic approaches, manuscript preparation, scientific communication, fellowship and grant writing, mentoring, and career development. The successful candidate will be encouraged to develop an independent scientific niche within the laboratory’s broader program in auditory genetics and functional genomics. Postdoctoral fellows will have opportunities to lead projects, establish collaborations, present research at national and international meetings, contribute to grant applications, and pursue individual fellowship or career-development funding. Indiana University School of Medicine provides a particularly strong environment for this work, with extensive expertise in human genetics, genomics, neuroscience, otolaryngology, auditory biology, genome editing, and translational medicine. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community, and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health and well-being throughout the state of Indiana. Department Contact for Questions Applicants should submit: Curriculum Vitae Letter of application, including brief statement of research interests and career goals Contact information for three references Through the Indiana University academic careers portal via this link https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34620 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Basic Qualifications Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in genetics, neuroscience, auditory biology, molecular biology, physiology, biomedical sciences, or a related discipline by the start of the appointment. Ability and willingness to work with mouse models are required for this position. Prior experience in inner-ear biology is highly desirable but is not required. Candidates from genetics, neuroscience, physiology, developmental biology, or related areas who are interested in developing expertise in auditory research are encouraged to apply. Experience in one or more of the following areas would be particularly valuable: ·         Mouse handling and experimental animal research. ·         Microsurgery or other technically demanding in vivo procedures. ·         Auditory or sensory physiology. ·         Mouse genetics. · CRISPR /Cas genome editing. · AAV or other gene-delivery approaches. ·         Histology, immunofluorescence, or confocal microscopy. ·         Molecular biology and gene-expression analysis. ·         Neuroscience or sensory-system biology. Training can be provided in specialized inner-ear techniques. Candidates should bring a strong experimental foundation, attention to technical detail, scientific curiosity, and an interest in developing increasing independence in experimental design and project leadership. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position and application materials may be directed to: Dr. Hela Azaiez, PhD (hazaiez@iu.edu) and Dr. Kevin T.A. Booth PhD, FACMG (kevbooth@iu.edu) Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02533-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34619": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medical & Molecular Genetics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated and creative Postdoctoral Fellow in Experimental Functional Genomics and Molecular Genetics to join the Azaiez-Booth Laboratory in Indianapolis, Indiana. Our laboratory studies how genetic variation causes human disease and develops experimental approaches to move from genetic discovery to biological mechanism. Research in the laboratory sits at the intersection of human genetics, molecular genetics, functional genomics, and precision genomic medicine, with particular interests in genetic hearing loss, RNA biology, variant interpretation, and the development of scalable functional assays. The successful candidate will take a leading role in our experimental functional genomics program, with opportunities to develop both high-throughput and gene-specific approaches for determining the functional consequences of human genetic variation. Current and developing projects include massively parallel splicing assays, functional characterization of coding and noncoding variants, development of new experimental strategies for genes that are poorly suited to existing assays, and mechanistic follow-up of candidate variants and genes emerging from human genomic studies. A major emphasis of this position is experimental problem solving and assay development. We are particularly interested in candidates with strong hands-on molecular biology skills who enjoy designing experiments, troubleshooting technically challenging systems, and developing new approaches. Expertise in one specific technology is less important than a strong experimental foundation and the ability to take a biological question from initial hypothesis through assay development, data generation, interpretation, and publication. Research Opportunities Depending on the project, projects may involve: ·         Development and implementation of high-throughput functional genomics assays. ·         Experimental characterization of variants affecting RNA splicing, gene expression, or protein function. ·         Massively parallel and sequencing-based functional assays. ·         Development of gene-specific assays for variants that cannot be adequately evaluated using existing platforms. ·         Molecular cloning, genome editing, cell-based functional studies, and quantitative molecular assays. ·         Integration of functional data with human genetic and clinical observations. ·         Mechanistic follow-up of genes and variants identified through human genomic studies. ·         Development of new experimental platforms that can be expanded into independent research directions. The fellow will be encouraged to develop scientific questions that complement the laboratory’s broader research program while establishing an increasingly independent area of expertise. Training and Career Development The Azaiez-Booth Laboratory is committed to helping postdoctoral fellows develop the scientific independence necessary for their next career stage. Fellows will receive mentorship in experimental design, manuscript preparation, scientific communication, grant and fellowship writing, mentoring junior trainees, and development of an independent research program. Postdoctoral fellows will be expected to lead research projects, contribute substantially to manuscripts and collaborative studies, present their work at scientific meetings, and participate in the preparation of fellowship and research grant applications. Fellows will also have opportunities to collaborate with investigators in human genetics, genomics, clinical genetics, hearing research, and other disciplines across Indiana University School of Medicine and collaborating institutions. We especially encourage applications from scientists who are interested in building a research program at the interface of human genetics and experimental biology and who want to develop new approaches for translating genetic observations into biological insight. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community, and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health and well-being throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in genetics, molecular biology, biochemistry, cell biology, biomedical sciences, or a related field by the start of the appointment. Ability and willingness to work with mouse models are required for this position. Candidates with experience in one or more of the following areas are particularly encouraged to apply: ·         Molecular cloning and construct design. · RNA biology or alternative splicing. ·         Mammalian cell culture and transfection. · CRISPR /Cas genome editing. ·         Next-generation sequencing-based assays. ·         Functional characterization of genetic variants. ·         High-throughput or multiplexed experimental approaches. ·         Quantitative analysis of molecular or cellular phenotypes. Applicants are not expected to have experience in all of these areas. More important are strong experimental skills, scientific curiosity, careful attention to experimental design, and a demonstrated ability to troubleshoot and independently advance research projects. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position and application materials may be directed to: Dr. Hela Azaiez, PhD (hazaiez@iu.edu) and Dr. Kevin T.A. Booth PhD, FACMG (kevbooth@iu.edu). Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02532-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34615": {
      "title": "Legal Communication and Analysis, Tenure-Track Faculty Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Legal Communication and Analysis, Tenure-Track Faculty Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Legal Communication and Analysis, Tenure-Track Faculty Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Legal Communication and Analysis, Tenure-Track Faculty Position Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Robert H. McKinney School of Law Location Indianapolis Position Summary INDIANA UNIVERSITY ROBERT H. MCKINNEY SCHOOL OF LAW invites applications from entry-level candidates (with 0-5 years experience) for full-time, tenure-track faculty positions teaching the required first-year Legal Communication and Analysis course during the 2027-28 academic year. The workload will be 40% teaching, 40% scholarship, and 20% service. The Indiana University McKinney School of Law offers a competitive salary and generous benefits, a reduced teaching package in the first year for entry-level candidates, a semester of pre-tenure research leave, a generous professional development account, funds to retain research assistants, and summer research grants for productive scholars. As the state’s premier urban research institution, IU Indianapolis is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. Indianapolis, where the law school is situated, is the state capital and a vibrant city with a wide variety of restaurants, theaters, and music venues; substantial outdoor green space; an award-winning international airport; and professional football, basketball, baseball ( AAA ), and soccer ( USL ) teams. Indianapolis regularly ranks near the top in listings of Best Affordable Places to live, according to independent rankings reported by MSN .com. Applications need to include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, three references, a writing sample (a recent scholarly article or work in progress), and teaching evaluations (if available). Basic Qualifications Applicants must hold a J.D. or equivalent degree with a record of academic excellence. We seek candidates with potential for or a record of innovative scholarship and engaged teaching. A recent scholarly publication or substantial work in progress is expected to evince scholarly aptitude and commitment. A judicial clerkship or practice experience with significant writing experience is strongly encouraged. Teaching experience is a plus. Department Contact for Questions For more information, please contact Professor Gerard Magliocca, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee, at gmaglioc@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101615-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Course Evaluation Writing Sample List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34614": {
      "title": "Tenured and Tenure-Track Professor of Law",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Tenured and Tenure-Track Professor of Law Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Tenured and Tenure-Track Professor of Law Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Tenured and Tenure-Track Professor of Law Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Robert H. McKinney School of Law Location Indianapolis Position Summary The INDIANA UNIVERSITY ROBERT H. MCKINNEY SCHOOL OF LAW invites applications from entry-level and lateral candidates for up to four full-time, tenured or tenure-track faculty positions to begin at the start of the 2027-28 academic year. The workload is 40% teaching, 40% scholarship, and 20% service. New hires will teach one or more required 1L course(s) and/or one or more core upper-level course(s). A successful candidate might expect to teach two or more of: Constitutional Law, Contracts, Criminal Law, Criminal Procedure, Evidence, Family Law, First Amendment Law, Introduction to Business Entities, Property, Torts, and Trusts & Estates as part of a four-course teaching package. The Indiana University McKinney School of Law offers a competitive salary and generous benefits, a three-course teaching package in the first year for pre-tenure hires, a semester of pre-tenure research leave, a generous professional development account, funds to retain research assistants, and summer research grants for productive scholars. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The culture of Indiana University Robert H. McKinney School of Law and of IU Indianapolis is of utmost importance. We dedicate ourselves to excellence in teaching, research, and service. All faculty members at Indiana University abide by the Principles of Ethical Conduct and AcademicAppointee Responsibilities and Conduct , and support the Code of Student Rights, Responsibilities, and Conduct and the institutional value of intellectual diversity . Faculty presence on campus is essential for the culture and eﬀectiveness of our school. Any requests for remote work must be for the beneﬁt of the school, approved by the dean each semester, and documented through a school process. Indianapolis, where the law school is situated, is the state capital and a vibrant city with a wide variety of restaurants, theaters, and music venues; substantial outdoor green space; an award-winning international airport; and professional football, basketball, baseball ( AAA ), and soccer ( USL ) teams. Indianapolis regularly ranks near the top in listings of Best Aﬀordable Places to live, according to independent rankings reported by MSN .com. To apply, please complete IU’s online application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33873 with a CV, letter of application, writing sample (a recent scholarly article or work in progress), list of three references, and any recent course evaluations. Basic Qualifications Applicants must hold a J.D. or equivalent degree with a record of academic excellence. We seek candidates with potential for or a record of innovative scholarship and engaged teaching. A recent scholarly publication or substantial work in progress is expected to evince scholarly aptitude and commitment. Prior practice experience is a plus. Department Contact for Questions For more information, please contact Professor Gerard Magliocca, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee, at gmaglioc@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101613-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Course Evaluation Writing Sample List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34599": {
      "title": "Chair Department of Physics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Chair Department of Physics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Chair Department of Physics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Chair Department of Physics Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Indianapolis Department of Physics – School of Science Location Indianapolis Position Summary Chair, Department of Physics, School of Science, IU Indianapolis Internal applicants only The IU-Indy Physics Department invites applications for the position of Chair of the Department of Physics from tenured candidates, particularly those at the full Professor rank. The Chair of the Department of Physics in the School of Science (SoS) at IU Indianapolis is expected to provide visionary leadership for the unit, including developing and executing a clear vision to strengthen the Department. The Chair is expected to attract, mentor, and retain a cohort of the highest quality faculty, staff and students, and to develop and maintain programs at both the undergraduate and graduate levels that highlight its strengths. The Chair will: · Develop a vision and execute a plan for strategic growth of the academic and research enterprises that align with the strategic plans of the SoS and IU Indianapolis. · Lead a team of academically strong  individuals and on issues including recruitment, retention, and management and development of faculty and staff. · Manage the departmental budget, keeping annual expenditure and income in balance. · Contribute to graduate and undergraduate teaching, and curriculum and program development by advancing and modernizing the curriculum. · Advance the Department’s research funding and reputation and support efforts to increase externally funded research. · Work collaboratively within the Department, the School of Science, and across IU Indianapolis. · ·Demonstrate talent in consensus building, strategic planning, vision, and innovation. · Demonstrate communication and interpersonal skills needed to be an effective leader. · Participate in the alumni relations and fundraising activities of the SoS. The successful candidate is expected to be an internationally recognized scholar in a sub-discipline of Physics, as evident from publication, citation, and funding records; possess a proven track record of service to the department and the SoS; have a successful record of teaching, research and creative activity, and service at IU Indianapolis; and have experience in working with multiple communities to broaden participation in science. Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae and a leadership statement. Review of applications will begin on Oct. 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Dr. Ruihua Cheng e-mail: rucheng@iu.edu Basic Qualifications Tenured Internal Physics Professor Department Contact for Questions Dr. Ruihua Cheng e-mail: rucheng@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Other – Leadership Statement third document to be submitted Salary and Rank Commensurate with rank and experience Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/15/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101718-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Other Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34593": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Open Rank), Indianapolis campus",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Lecturer (Open Rank), Indianapolis campus Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Lecturer (Open Rank), Indianapolis campus Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The School of Nursing is committed to being a welcoming institution. We seek applicants whose research, teaching, and service efforts contribute to learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. Open positions are available for January 2027 start dates. Letter of application, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching and contact list with three references must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34549 For additional information, please contact Betsy Holtel @ eabraun@iu.edu or Julie Otte @ jlelam@iu.edu. Posting Details Position Details Title Lecturer (Open Rank), Indianapolis campus Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Nursing Location Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Nursing seeks qualified candidates for lecturer positions at the Indiana University Indianapolis campus. The lecturers would be responsible for teaching clinical and/or didactic courses in the undergraduate program and other courses as appropriate. We are searching for applicants with expertise in leadership, healthy populations/public health, obstetrics, psychiatric mental health, critical care, and med/surg experience. Scholarship of teaching and service are expected to advance on the lecturer track. Overall, the candidate’s responsibilities include contributing to the School of Nursing’s teaching, and service missions. 1. The candidate is expected to engage in high-quality teaching. The candidate will leverage teaching strategies to create learning spaces as well as to prepare our graduates to enter and lead in an increasingly complex world. 2. The candidate is expected to participate in service at the school, campus, community, and professional levels. Basic Qualifications Qualified applicants must hold a master’s degree in nursing. Recent clinical and/or teaching experience is preferred. The successful candidate must be licensed or eligible for Indiana RN licensure. Appointment to academic rank and salary are commensurate with prior education, experience, skills, and training. Department Contact for Questions Astrid Mejia 317-274-7695 Tammy Leone 317-274-4157 Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions The School of Nursing is committed to being a welcoming institution. We seek applicants whose research, teaching, and service efforts contribute to learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. Open positions are available for January 2027 start dates. Letter of application, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching and contact list with three references must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34549 For additional information, please contact Betsy Holtel @ eabraun@iu.edu or Julie Otte @ jlelam@iu.edu. For Best Consideration Date 10/23/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101714-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34589": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Appointee",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Post Doctoral Appointee Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Post Doctoral Appointee Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Post Doctoral Appointee Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Microbiology & Immunology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary An NIH -funded postdoctoral position is available immediately in the laboratory of Dr. Laura Snell at the Indiana University School of Medicine in Indianapolis, Indiana. The laboratory studies the molecular and cellular regulation of T cell responses during chronic viral infections and cancers, and how immunotherapy works to restore these exhausted T cells. We have a particular interest in CD4 T cell responses during these chronic diseases. This includes understanding the nature of their helper functions, as well as the capacity of CD4 T cells for direct cytotoxic killing. This postdoctoral position will follow up on our previous work (Snell et al. Cell Reports 2016, Snell et al. Nat Immunol. 2021) and use mouse models and cutting-edge technologies to examine the mechanisms by which CD4 T cells accelerate the clearance of chronic viral infections and tumors. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications A recent PhD in immunology, virology, cell biology or a related field with a solid publication track record including at least 1 first author publication in a peer-reviewed journal. Department Contact for Questions Dr. Laura Snell ( lausnell@iu.edu ) Additional Qualifications 1)    Be highly motivated with a genuine interest and curiosity for science 2)    Have a strong understanding of immunology 3)    Proficiency in multicolor / spectral flow cytometry is required. 4)    Experience with mouse handling, infection or cancer models is preferred, but not required. 5)    Experience analyzing NGS data (scRNA-seq etc.) is an asset, but not required. 6)    Ability to work independently as well as collegially within a team Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02529-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34578": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor in Convergent Biology and Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Associate or Full Professor in Convergent Biology and Biomedical Engineering Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Associate or Full Professor in Convergent Biology and Biomedical Engineering Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Associate or Full Professor in Convergent Biology and Biomedical Engineering Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Indianapolis Department of Biomedical Engineering and Informatics –  Luddy School Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University (IU) School of Science and the Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering at IU Indianapolis invite applications for a tenured Associate or Full Professor position whose research operates at the intersection of biology and biomedical engineering. This position is a strategic joint hire aligned with the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) and is intended to strengthen and expand interdisciplinary research capacity across biology, biomedical engineering, and translational biosciences. Strategic Research Areas of Interest Priority consideration will be given to candidates whose research programs align with one or more of the following areas: - Bacterial pathogenesis and antibiotic resistance, including microbiology, immune engineering, and host–pathogen interactions - Biohybrid and bioinspired materials and biological–synthetic interfaces - Bio-machining, bio-manufacturing, and biofabrication, including bioprinting and scalable living systems - Regenerative biology and regenerative medicine with translational relevance Role within the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) The Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) drives interdisciplinary bioscience and technology research and collaboration with industry partners in pursuit of discoveries ranging from wearable and implantable medical devices and sensors to the advanced detection of biomarkers and AI-enabled drug discovery and delivery. With a goal of quickly delivering IU health and sciences innovations from the bench to bedside, the institute will aim to stimulate substantial growth in the disclosure and licensing of inventions, discoveries and innovations, as well as industry partnerships and IU-launched startup companies. This position is affiliated with the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ), IU Indianapolis’ institute dedicated to advancing convergent bioscience and technology research. Faculty affiliated with CBATI are expected to participate actively in interdisciplinary research efforts and collaborative proposal development. IU has also recently broken ground on the Launch Accelerator for Biosciences (IU LAB ) – a first-of-its kind academic-industry initiative to harness Indiana’s extensive resources in bioscience discovery, leading to significant advancements in human health. Supported by a $138 million Lilly Endowment Inc. grant, IU LAB acts as the hub between industry and academia, positioning Indiana and the greater Indianapolis region as an international leader for biosciences research and innovation. IU LAB is expected to open in the 16 Tech Innovation District in 2027. The successful candidate will have opportunities to contribute to shaping collaborative research themes, support interdisciplinary training and mentoring, and engage with translational and industry-facing initiatives through CBATI and IU LAB , consistent with their interests and experience. Application Process Open until filled. Application materials include: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Statement of Research Statement of Teaching List of References About the Indiana University Luddy School of Informatics, Computing and Engineering – Indianapolis The Indiana University Luddy School of Informatics, Computing and Engineering is the first completely new school in the United States devoted exclusively to Informatics and a range of its subdisciplines. With its formative national role in creating the nation’s largest Informatics Program on the Bloomington and Indianapolis campuses, the school is the broadest and one of the largest information/computing schools in the U.S. At IU Indianapolis, the school also has strong ties with the health and life sciences in the areas of health data exchange, clinical decision support, consumer health informatics, integrated health information systems, and interactive health information technologies. The school provides state-of-the-art facilities including fully equipped classrooms, media and gaming labs, human–computer interaction research labs, usability and mobile development labs, ample research facilities, and studios for sound design and interactive media production. Access to advanced, high-resolution wall-sized displays and virtual environments are also available within the Informatics and Communications Technology Complex. The school has formed key research partnerships with the IU School of Medicine and the Regenstrief Institute, an internationally recognized medical informatics research center. The school also enjoys collaborations with the Roudebush VA Medical Center, IU Health (one of the largest health care organizations in the Midwest), the IU Schools of Nursing, Public Health, Health and Human Sciences, and School of Science. Luddy School faculty have access to state-of-the art computing resources. IU’s Quartz supercomputer, Big Red 200 is among the world’s fastest research supercomputers. Owned and operated solely by IU, these high-performance resources are designed to accelerate discovery in a wide variety of fields, including biomedicine, biotechnology, health care, and enable effective analysis of large, complex biomedical data sets (i.e., big data). About the Indiana University School of Science The School of Science is home to six departments and two interdisciplinary academic programs that serve over 2000 students. Each of the six departments hosts thriving PhD and research programs. The Department of Biology has 25 full-time faculty members, over 750 undergraduate majors, and approximately 60 graduate students enrolled in Ph.D. and M.S. programs. The Department of Biology has a strong record of externally funded research, well-equipped research laboratories and several core facilities. With an extensive hospital system and the IU School of Medicine, IU Indianapolis is home to a large and vibrant community of researchers and collaborators in all areas of biomedical science. Consistent with our health and life science focus, many faculty in the department have strong connections with the IU School of Medicine ( Indiana University School of Medicine (iu.edu) ) on the same campus, and pursue strong, externally funded, programmatic research. About Indiana University Indianapolis The IU Indianapolis campus, with over 21,000 students, is located adjacent to downtown Indianapolis. IU Indianapolis is the health and life science campus of Indiana University, the focal point of health profession education in the State of Indiana. IU Indianapolis offers a full range of academic programs, and is an academic leader in the development and use of information technology. IU Indianapolis shares a campus with the Indiana University School of Medicine, the largest medical school in the US. IU Indianapolis is an eight-time recipient of the Higher Education Excellence in Diversity Award by Insight into Diversity. IU Indianapolis is a campus renowned for service learning, civic engagement, and health sciences research. About Indianapolis Indianapolis is the nation’s 16th largest city, the capital of Indiana, home to the Indianapolis Motor Speedway, the NCAA , the Indianapolis Colts and Indiana Pacers, Indiana Repertory Theatre, Indianapolis Symphony, Indianapolis Ballet, Indianapolis Museum of Art, Indianapolis Zoo and one of the country’s most livable big cities. Indiana is entering a new era of dynamic growth led by the biosciences, manufacturing, artificial intelligence, healthcare and hospitality industries. Just last year, Indiana’s biosciences sector generated over $99 billion in economic activity, while its hospitality industry welcomed 30 million visitors who contributed $5.8 billion of economic impact. Indianapolis is a significant biotech hub, anchored by global giants like Eli Lilly and Roche, alongside numerous other major players like Elanco, AstraZeneca, Merck, and Sanofi, plus specialized firms such as Zimmer Biomet, INCOG BioPharma, Genezen Labs, and Beckman Coulter, making it a strong center for pharmaceuticals, diagnostics, and life sciences. To learn more about Indianapolis, see any of the following websites: IndyParks , with over 150 parks in and around the city Local Government & Neighborhoods Downtown Indianapolis Tourism Indianapolis Monthly’s “Best of Indy” Carmel, Indiana , rated the #1 Best Place to Live in the U.S. Basic Qualifications Basic Qualifications Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, Biotechnology, or a closely related discipline Appointment at the tenured Associate or Full Professor level at time of hire Demonstrated ability to sustain an externally funded research program Evidence of effective teaching and mentoring. Department Contact for Questions Dr. James Lin, Director, cyjlin@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 08/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101454-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy Research Agenda List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34574": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor in Biostatistics and Health Data Science",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Research Professor in Biostatistics and Health Data Science Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Research Professor in Biostatistics and Health Data Science Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Research Professor in Biostatistics and Health Data Science Specific Title Appointment Type Scientist Track Faculty Department IUSM - Biostatistics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Faculty position in biostatistics, health data science, and informatics areas available in the Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science ( BHDS ), associated with the Indiana University School of Medicine and Fairbanks School of Public Health on the Indianapolis campus. Position level is Assistant Research Professor/Non-Tenure track. This is an exciting opportunity to join a fast growing BHDS Department with 36 faculty members and more than 50 professional staff. We are dedicated to excellence in biostatistical, health data science, and informatics research and education. We have an extensive portfolio of research program in related areas. Our NIH funding as principal investigators and co-investigators has been ranked among the top in the nation. The Department currently has an ongoing PhD program in Biostatistics, MS program in Biostatistics, and BS program in Health Data Science. We have established strong collaborations with both clinical and basic science research departments and divisions and health systems including IU Health and Eskenazi Hospitals as well as strong partnerships with major research institutes and centers including Regenstrief Institute, Center for Computational Biology and Bioinformatics, Indiana Clinical and Translational Science Institute ( CTSI ), Indiana University Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center, Indiana Institute of Biomedical Research, and Indiana Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center. Additional details about the Department and the PhD program are available on our web page: https://medicine.iu.edu/biostatistics. The position will focus on biomedical informatics, multimodal artificial intelligence, medical imaging, biomedical data modeling, and software/data engineering for large collaborative biomedical research programs. The successful candidate will participate in interdisciplinary research involving multimodal biomedical data integration, medical imaging, graph-based and AI modeling, biomedical informatics, and development of research software and data platforms. The position is expected to contribute to large collaborative federally funded research programs and to work closely with investigators across biomedical, clinical, and data science disciplines. The Indianapolis Campus is the focal point of health professions education at Indiana University, and the School of Medicine is the country’s second largest allopathic medical school. Indianapolis consistently ranks high nationally on many of the “best places to live” lists and has an economy that is growing in the life sciences arena. In addition, it has always been one of the cities with the lowest cost of living. Carmel, Indy’s northern neighbor, was recently named as the best mid-sized city in the country. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation The search will continue until the positions are filled. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or Terminal degree is required. Quals: PhD in Biostatistics, Requirements include a Ph.D. (or equivalent) in biostatistics, health data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, informatics, data science, computer science, electrical engineering, mathematics, or related fields. Department Contact for Questions Jing Su, PhD Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02524-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34572": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - – Health Equity, Health Disparities, and Community-Engaged Research",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow - – Health Equity, Health Disparities, and Community-Engaged Research Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow - – Health Equity, Health Disparities, and Community-Engaged Research Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Professional Development Opportunities The fellow will receive: • Individual mentorship from Dr. Amy Knopf. • Access to CEQL research seminars and scholarly activities. • Opportunities to participate in interdisciplinary collaborations across Indiana University. • Rigorous research training • Grant-writing and career development support. • Support for conference presentations and professional networking. • Guidance in developing competitive career development and research funding applications. Additional Information The postdoctoral position is full-time with a compensation commensurate with the National Research Service Awards (NRSA) stipend levels plus an additional $10,000.00 awarded by the IU School of Nursing. The appointment is for one year, with the possibility of a one-year extension contingent upon performance and available funding. Interested applicants should submit the following materials: 1. Curriculum vitae 2. Statement of research interests and qualifications (maximum 2 pages) 3. Three examples of scholarly written work 4. Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow - – Health Equity, Health Disparities, and Community-Engaged Research Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Nursing Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Nursing seeks a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join the Center for Enhancing Quality of Life in Chronic Illness ( CEQL ). The fellow will work under the mentorship of Dr. Amy Knopf, an established nurse scientist whose research focuses on health equity, health disparities, social determinants of health, bioethics, and clinical trial access. This fellowship is designed to support the development of an early-career nurse scientist pursuing an independent program of research focused on health equity, health disparities, ethics, and/or population health. The fellow will engage in ongoing research projects while receiving individualized mentorship in research design, data analysis, scientific writing, grantsmanship, community engagement, and academic career development. The Center for Enhancing Quality of Life in Chronic Illness ( CEQL ) is dedicated to advancing research that improves the quality of life of individuals living with chronic illness. CEQL fosters interdisciplinary collaboration, mentorship, and innovation to address complex health challenges through rigorous scientific inquiry, implementation science, and community engagement. The fellowship provides an opportunity to collaborate with interdisciplinary researchers, community organizations, health systems, and policy partners across Indiana University and beyond while contributing to innovative research that addresses health disparities and promotes equitable health outcomes. Research Focus Areas • Health equity and health disparities • Social determinants of health • Community-engaged and participatory research • Ethics • Clinical trial access, enrollment, and retention • Policy Primary Responsibilities of the Fellow • Develop an individualized career development plan leading toward research independence and external funding. • Conduct independent and collaborative research activities. • Work with faculty mentor on ongoing projects, including: study design, implementation, data collection, and analysis. • Prepare manuscripts for submission to peer-reviewed journals. • Present research at IU School of Nursing Research Forums and at regional and national professional conferences. Basic Qualifications This position is on-site and located in Indianapolis, Indiana. The fellow will be expected to be on campus at least three days/week. Required Qualifications Completion of a PhD in Nursing or an aligned health field within the last five years. Note the PhD must be conferred prior to the start date for this position on 1/1/27. Must be authorized to work in the United States for the duration of the appointment without requiring employer-sponsored visa sponsorship. This position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Demonstrated interest in the research focus areas described above. Commitment to a research-focused career in an academic, public sector, or health care environment. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary and community-based research environments. Demonstrated interest in research focus areas above. • Commitment to a research-focused career in an academic, public sector, or health care environment. • Strong written and verbal communication. • Ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary and community-based research environments. Preferred Qualifications • Record of peer-reviewed publications. • Experience designing and conducting quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods research. • Experience working with community partners or stakeholder-engaged research. • Experience in population health or health equity/disparities research. Department Contact for Questions For questions about the position, please contact Jared Rutan jmrutan@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Professional Development Opportunities The fellow will receive: • Individual mentorship from Dr. Amy Knopf. • Access to CEQL research seminars and scholarly activities. • Opportunities to participate in interdisciplinary collaborations across Indiana University. • Rigorous research training • Grant-writing and career development support. • Support for conference presentations and professional networking. • Guidance in developing competitive career development and research funding applications. Additional Information The postdoctoral position is full-time with a compensation commensurate with the National Research Service Awards ( NRSA ) stipend levels plus an additional $10,000.00 awarded by the IU School of Nursing. The appointment is for one year, with the possibility of a one-year extension contingent upon performance and available funding. Interested applicants should submit the following materials: 1. Curriculum vitae 2. Statement of research interests and qualifications (maximum 2 pages) 3. Three examples of scholarly written work 4. Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101626-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Research Paper 1 Research Paper 2 Research Paper 3 Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34567": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor of Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor of Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor of Pediatrics Specific Title Appointment Type Tenure Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Herman B Wells Center for Pediatric Research conducts basic science and translational research within the Department of Pediatrics at Indiana University School of Medicine. The Wells Center houses more than 200 faculty investigators, staff, and trainees seeking answers to the most pressing questions related to childhood illness. With the collaboration between scientists and physicians, the center aims to increase knowledge of causes and mechanisms of serious pediatric diseases. Our goal is to develop innovative approaches to the diagnosis and treatment of sick children in Indiana and beyond. The Pediatric Cardiac Developmental Biology Research group in the Herman B Wells Center at Indiana University School of Medicine in Indianapolis is accepting applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the Assistant Professor rank. Scientific focus should be on Cardiac development, congenital heart disease and/or IPS related cardiac models of differentiation/ cell regeneration. Employment of zebrafish and mouse models is also considered a strength. The scientific environment at the School of Medicine and in Herman B Wells Center for Pediatric Research is highly interactive and collegial with and includes interactions with clinician in the Department of Pediatrics. Further interactions on campus include other Basic Science and Clinical Departments, as well as Research Centers and Institutes. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree required. Candidates should hold a Ph.D., M.D., or M.D./Ph.D. and demonstrate a record of productive and high-quality research. Department Contact for Questions Anthony Firulli PhD tfirulli@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02527-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Application Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34556": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Radiology, Pediatric Neuroradiology, Indianapolis",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate Professor of Radiology, Pediatric Neuroradiology, Indianapolis Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate Professor of Radiology, Pediatric Neuroradiology, Indianapolis Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Radiology, Pediatric Neuroradiology, Indianapolis Specific Title Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Radiology & Imaging Sciences Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary INDIANA UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF MEDICINE DEPARTMENT OF RADIOLOGY AND IMAGING SCIENCES Indiana University Health PEDIATRIC NEURORADIOLOGIST FACULTY POSITION • PEDIATRIC NEURORADIOLOGY • INDIANAPOLIS , INDIANA This position is eligible for a signing bonus of up to $100,000! The Indiana University School of Medicine Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences invites applications for a fellowship-trained Pediatric Neuroradiologist to join the Pediatric Neuroradiology Division, at Riley Hospital for Children. This is an opportunity to practice the full spectrum of pediatric diagnostic neuroradiology at one of the nation’s premier academic medical centers, with provision to balance clinical and academic time. The position may be at the assistant, associate, or full professor level based on experience, with both tenured and non-tenure track appointments available. Practice Highlights • Pediatric neuroradiology coverage is based at Riley Hospital for Children in Indianapolis, ranked among the top children’s hospitals in the nation. • Daytime position with approximately one remote evening shift per week and no overnight call responsibilities. • Work-from-home opportunities shared among the Pediatric Neuroradiology team, in addition to on-site assignments. • Opportunities for cross-division appointment (i.e. Pediatric Radiology, Adult Neuroradiology, Nuclear Medicine, etc). • Work alongside nationally recognized experts in Neuroradiology, supported by a robust residency, a neuroradiology fellowship program, and a highly supportive, well-funded imaging sciences research program. Why IU? • Interpret all diagnostic pediatric neuroradiology imaging studies on a collegial and growing team. • Participate in multidisciplinary tumor boards and specialty conferences, collaborating closely with referring clinicians. • Be a part of the largest medical school in the nation. • New medical school campus and research buildings opened in downtown Indianapolis in 2025. • Practice at Riley Hospital for Children at IU Health, Indiana’s only nationally ranked children’s hospital and one of only 21 in the country ranked by US News & World Report in all 11 pediatric specialties. • Our department ranked 1st among Big Ten schools and 13th nationally in overall NIH funding by the Academy for Radiology & Biomedical Imaging Research in 2024. • Collaborate with the newly established departmental Medical Imaging Research Institute ( MIRI ), including the Cancer Imaging and Theranostics Research Center, Research Imaging Core, and state-of-the-art PET /CT, PET /MR, and preclinical imaging infrastructure. • Benefit from a strong dual-employment partnership between IU School of Medicine and IU Health, which provides access to the latest research, training, clinical care, and educational opportunities. Compensation & Benefits • Total compensation (salary + benefits) is estimated between $610,000-$650,000 depending on academic rank, with ample opportunities for supplemental income. • This position is eligible for a signing bonus of up to $100,000. • Balanced scheduling that supports personal well-being: daytime coverage with around one evening per week and no overnight call. • Flexible work model with both on-site and shared work-from-home reading opportunities. • Comprehensive benefits package through Indiana University, one of the state’s largest employers. • Dual-employment offers unique investment opportunities (401(k) plus 457(b) and 403(b), dual Roth contributions, HSA rollover). • Tuition benefit for dependent students attending Indiana University. • Relocation assistance and dual-career support. Vibrant City, Exceptional Suburbs Indianapolis offers a thriving downtown and family-friendly suburbs. Many faculty choose to live in Carmel, Zionsville, and Fishers, which are frequently ranked among the best places to live nationally. The region combines top-rated public and private schools, affordable housing, and cultural amenities with a strong life sciences economy. For additional information on life in Indy, please visit click here. RESPONSIBILITIES • Provide high-quality clinical interpretation of the full spectrum of pediatric diagnostic neuroradiology studies at Riley Hospital for Children and coverage of multiple in-state hospitals and imaging facilities. • Participate in multidisciplinary tumor boards and specialty conferences and contribute to educational initiatives across the department and Academic Health Center. • Teach and mentor medical students, residents, and fellows in a supportive academic environment. • Engage in scholarly activity, quality improvement, and faculty development opportunities aligned with the department’s four missions—clinical, research, education, and culture. HOW TO APPLY • Please see the online application: HERE • Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest, current CV, and references in PDF format. • For questions regarding the position and application process, please contact Dr. Brian Graner. • For more information regarding our institution, please see the department, division, and IU School of Medicine websites. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS • MD or DO degree (doctorate or terminal degree required). • Board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Radiology. • Fellowship training in Pediatric Neuroradiology. • Eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana. • Excellent interpersonal skills and qualities that further the clinical, academic, and research missions of the department. • A track record and commitment to education and scholarship in at least one mission area is expected. Research experience and grant funding are assets but not required Department Contact for Questions Carla Ewing Associate Director, HR, IUSM Radiology & Imaging Sciences carewing@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02525-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34553": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Ophthalmology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Ophthalmology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Ophthalmology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Ophthalmology Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Ophthalmology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Eugene and Marilyn Glick Eye Institute at the Indiana University School of Medicine invites applications for a Postdoctoral Fellowship position in the Department of Ophthalmology. Our research focus is retinal cell biology with the goal of understanding the fundamental processes of vision and discovering small molecule therapy for photoreceptor degenerative disorders. To elucidate the disease mechanisms, we genetically dissect the molecular mechanisms underlying photoreceptor morphogenesis and maintenance in vertebrate animals. Our laboratory pioneered real-time imaging of proteins and small molecules within the retina and takes advantage of state of the art confocal/super resolution microscopy techniques. Our research also heavily depends on the tissue culture system to biochemically study the components required for photoreceptor morphogenesis/maintenance. The applicant is expected to be highly independent and be capable of designing experiments and conducting hypothesis-based research projects. The candidate is expected to be familiar with basic laboratory operations and proper documentation of experimental records. The candidate should have a strong background in cell biology, molecular biology, or biochemistry. Knowledge of fluorescence microscopy is favorable, although not necessary. The Glick Eye Institute is located on the IU School of Medicine campus in downtown Indianapolis. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Salary will be commensurate with experience and will adhere to current Indiana University School of Medicine guidelines. Applicants should submit a cover letter summarizing their relevant qualifications, and a copy of their curriculum vitae. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree required. Department Contact for Questions Inquiries about the position may be sent to Dr. Yoshikazu Imanishi, Department of Ophthalmology, yimanish@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02523-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34493": {
      "title": "Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter describing research vision and leadership experience, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Posting Details Position Details Title Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Specific Title Assistant/Associate/Professor of Psychiatry Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Psychiatry Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) and the Indiana University Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center ( IUSCCC ) invite applications for a nationally recognized investigator to serve as Director of Research for the Walther Center for Supportive Oncology. This open-rank faculty position offers an exceptional opportunity to shape a growing, endowed, interdisciplinary research program at Indiana’s only National Cancer Institute-designated comprehensive cancer center. The Director of Research will help define the next generation of supportive oncology science – research that improves the cancer experience for patients and families from diagnosis through treatment, survivorship, serious illness, and end-of-life care. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative environment with substantial institutional commitment, endowed-chair support, access to a broad clinical enterprise, and opportunities to integrate research, clinical care, education, and community engagement. Why this opportunity is compelling · Endowed platform: The position is supported by one of five Walther-endowed chairs established to recruit academic leaders in supportive oncology. · Program-building scope: The Director will have a visible leadership role in expanding, integrating, and sustaining supportive oncology research across the cancer center, IUSM , IU Health, and community partners. · Integrated research and care environment: The Walther Center encompasses both research and clinical care, creating opportunities to embed discovery into real-world oncology practice. · Flexible academic home: The primary department and faculty rank will be aligned with the candidate’s expertise, credentials, and scholarly discipline. · Clinical flexibility: Opportunities for limited clinical practice may be available for appropriately trained candidates and tailored to expertise and interests. · Statewide and national impact: IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and community partners offer opportunities for team science, implementation, dissemination, and health equity-focused research across a wide variety of populations. Research focus areas We welcome investigators whose work advances supportive oncology across the cancer continuum and across the lifespan including children, adolescents/young adults, and adults. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: · Psycho-oncology, psychosocial and behavioral oncology, cancer-related distress, resilience, and mental health · Palliative care, symptom science, patient-reported outcomes, communication, decision-making, and serious illness care · Survivorship, cancer and aging, caregiver and family outcomes, sexual health, and quality of life · Integrative oncology, rehabilitation, physical activity, exercise and lifestyle interventions, nutrition, music therapy, and mind-body interventions · Implementation science, dissemination, health services research, digital health, health equity, rural health, and community-engaged research Leadership responsibilities · Set and advance a strategic research vision for the Walther Center for Supportive Oncology in partnership with center and cancer center leadership. · Build collaborative, cross-disciplinary research programs that leverage the strengths of IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and affiliated clinical and community partners. · Grow a high-impact portfolio of peer-reviewed extramural funding, publications, clinical and translational research, and dissemination initiatives. · Mentor faculty, trainees, and research staff; support career development for emerging investigators in supportive oncology. · Strengthen research infrastructure, including team-science models, data resources, patient-reported outcomes, pragmatic trials, and learning health system approaches. · Represent the Walther Center locally and nationally through scholarly leadership, partnerships, and visibility in supportive oncology research communities. About the Walther Center and IU Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center The Walther Center for Supportive Oncology is a major initiative of IUSM and IUSCCC , supported through transformational investment from the Walther Cancer Foundation and developed in partnership with IU Health. The center brings together complementary strengths in palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, symptom management, and survivorship to improve care and outcomes for people with cancer and their families. IUSCCC is Indiana’s only NCI -designated comprehensive cancer center and a member of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. Its investigators conduct basic, clinical, translational, population, and community-engaged research with a mission to reduce cancer mortality and suffering through discovery, education, and patient-centered care. About IU School of Medicine and Indianapolis IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States, with a statewide academic and clinical footprint and a strong record of research funding, education, and partnership. IU Indianapolis is located in the heart of a growing life sciences region with major health care, pharmaceutical, biotechnology, and innovation partners. Indianapolis offers the advantages of a major metropolitan area – professional sports, arts and culture, restaurants, trails, parks, festivals, and an accessible international airport – with a cost of living and quality of life that support recruitment and retention. The region includes nationally recognized communities such as Carmel, Fishers, and Noblesville, along with a wide range of urban, suburban, and rural living options. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications · PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant discipline. · An established, independently funded research program with a sustained record of peer-reviewed extramural funding and scholarly productivity. · National or international recognition in supportive oncology or a closely related field. · Demonstrated success leading interdisciplinary research initiatives and collaborating across clinical, academic, and community settings. · Record of mentoring learners, junior faculty, and/or multidisciplinary research teams. · Commitment to advancing inclusive excellence, health equity, and patient- and family-centered cancer care through research, teaching, mentoring, service, or community engagement. Department Contact for Questions psychr@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter describing research vision and leadership experience, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Priority Application Review Deadline 10/20/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02503-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Other Other 2 Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Other 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34492": {
      "title": "Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Posting Details Position Details Title Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Specific Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Psychiatry Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Psychiatry Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) and the Indiana University Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center ( IUSCCC ) invite applications for Walther Center Endowed Chair for Supportive Oncology position. This open-rank faculty position is designed for an investigator who is ready to grow a high-impact, collaborative research program in an environment with substantial institutional commitment, endowed-chair support, and access to Indiana’s only National Cancer Institute-designated comprehensive cancer center. The Walther Center brings together research and clinical care to improve the cancer experience for patients and families. The successful candidate will join a growing team of scholars and clinicians working across palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, survivorship, symptom science, patient-reported outcomes, health services research, and related fields. Why this opportunity is compelling · Endowed support: The chair is one of five Walther-endowed positions created to recruit academic leaders and accelerate supportive oncology research and care. · Collaborative environment: Candidates will engage with investigators, clinicians, trainees, IU Health partners, and community collaborators across a highly interdisciplinary academic health system. · Flexible fit: Faculty rank, department home, and clinical effort, where applicable, will be aligned with the candidate’s discipline, expertise, and career goals. · Growth orientation: The center is building a long-term initiative with opportunities for new programs, team science, pragmatic and translational research, implementation, and dissemination. · Mission and impact: The role offers a platform to improve quality of life, care delivery, health equity, and outcomes for people with cancer and their loved ones. Research focus areas We welcome applicants from a wide range of disciplines whose scholarship advances supportive oncology across the cancer continuum and across the lifespan including children, adolescents/young adults, and adults. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: · Psycho-oncology, behavioral oncology, mental health, coping, distress, resilience, and caregiver/family support · Palliative care, symptom management, cancer pain, fatigue, cachexia, sleep, communication, decision-making, and serious illness care · Survivorship, cancer and aging, sexual health, quality of life, rehabilitation, return-to-work and functional outcomes · Integrative oncology, exercise, physical activity, lifestyle interventions, nutrition, music therapy, and mind-body interventions · Health services research, implementation science, dissemination, digital health, patient-reported outcomes, health equity, and community-engaged research Responsibilities · Maintain and expand an independent, peer-reviewed research program in supportive oncology or a closely related field. · Develop innovative, collaborative, and translational scholarship that contributes to the growth and national visibility of the Walther Center. · Pursue external funding and publish high-impact scholarly work commensurate with rank and discipline. · Participate in multidisciplinary and team-science initiatives across IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and community partners. · Mentor learners, staff, trainees, and/or junior colleagues, commensurate with career stage and appointment level. · Contribute to teaching, service, leadership, and community engagement activities consistent with the candidate’s expertise and faculty appointment. About the Walther Center and IU Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center The Walther Center for Supportive Oncology is a major initiative of IUSM and IUSCCC , supported by the Walther Cancer Foundation and developed in partnership with IU Health. The center integrates palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, symptom management, survivorship, research, and clinical care to improve the lives of people with cancer and their families. IUSCCC is Indiana’s only NCI -designated comprehensive cancer center and a member of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. Cancer center investigators work across basic, clinical, translational, population, and community-engaged research, with substantial institutional resources to support research development, collaboration, and scientific innovation. About IU School of Medicine and Indianapolis IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States, with campuses across Indiana and a mission to advance health through education, research, and patient-centered care. IU Indianapolis is located in a growing life sciences region with strong academic, health care, philanthropic, community, and industry partnerships. Indianapolis combines the amenities of a major city with an accessible, livable metropolitan region. Candidates will find professional sports, arts and culture, restaurants, parks, trails, festivals, and diverse neighborhoods, as well as nearby communities such as Carmel, Fishers, Noblesville, and Zionsville that are frequently recognized for quality of life. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications · PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant discipline. · A productive independent research program with peer-reviewed extramural funding or a strong funding trajectory appropriate to rank. · Scholarly productivity and an emerging, established, or nationally recognized reputation in supportive oncology or a related field. · Demonstrated commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration across clinical, research, educational, and/or community settings. · Experience mentoring learners, staff, research teams, or junior colleagues, commensurate with rank and career stage. · Commitment to advancing inclusive excellence, health equity, and patient- and family-centered cancer care through research, teaching, mentoring, service, or community engagement. Department Contact for Questions psychr@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Priority Application Review Deadline 11/23/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02502-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Other Other 2 Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34490": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant or Associate Professor of Occupational Therapy Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant or Associate Professor of Occupational Therapy Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant or Associate Professor of Occupational Therapy Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Occupational Therapy – School of Health & Human Sciences Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Occupational Therapy Program in the Department of Graduate Health Professions, School of Health & Human Sciences ( SHHS ) at Indiana University – Indianapolis invites applications for a 10-month tenure-track faculty position. The preferred start date is August 2027; however, an earlier start date may be considered based on candidate availability. Salary will be commensurate with experience and academic credentials. The successful applicant will demonstrate the ability to teach and mentor both entry-level and post-professional OTD students. The Tenure rank faculty position will also be responsible for developing an active research agenda in their area of expertise, generate external funding, engage in online and classroom teaching, and provide school and university service. Duties for the Assistant or Associate Professor Position: 40% Teaching 50% Research 10% Service/Engagement The Occupational Therapy Program ranked by US News & World Report as #1 in the state and 23rd in the nation is part of a dynamic academic and research community that includes four programs in the Department of Graduate Health Professions (Occupational Therapy, Physical Therapy, Nutrition and Dietetics, and Physician Assistant) eight departments in the School of Health and Human Sciences ( SHHS ), 15 research labs and institutes, and over 400 clinical and community partnerships. IU Indianapolis is a leading urban research university located in downtown Indianapolis, serving more than 25,000 students across a wide range of programs. The campus includes over a dozen schools and academic units, offering degrees in fields such as business, engineering, health sciences, and the arts. A major highlight is its world-renowned Life Sciences campus, one of the largest in the country, where students learn alongside top medical professionals and researchers at facilities like hospitals, labs, and research centers. Through hands-on learning and strong community partnerships, IU Indianapolis prepares students to make a meaningful impact in their careers and in Indiana’s growing economy. IU Indianapolis has earned national recognition for its innovative health and life sciences initiatives, providing extensive opportunities for collaborative, interdisciplinary research. The university is making significant investments in research, including the launch of two new institutes: the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) and the Institute for Human Health and Wellbeing (H2W). Additionally, a $138 million grant from the Lilly Endowment will support the IU LAB , a state-of-the-art facility at the 16 Tech site designed to position Indiana as a national leader in bioscience discovery, commercialization, and talent development. SHHS seeks scholars who will contribute to our mission through excellence in teaching, research, and service and who value the perspectives and experiences represented across our campus, community, and profession. We are committed to fostering an inclusive academic environment that supports student learning, collaboration, and professional success. Application review will begin October 30, 2026 . Applications received by this date will receive priority consideration. Application materials should include: a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching and research philosophy, and names/contact information for three references. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . Individuals who require a reasonable accommodation to participate in the search process must notify the School’s HR Business Partner, Myrna Palacios, at mypala@iu.edu, in advance. Basic Qualifications An earned PhD in a field relevant to occupational therapy, including but not limited to rehabilitation science, public health, behavioral health, or health education. Potential for success in research as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications and grant funding. Evolving program of research aligned with or complementary to the School of Health & Human Sciences. Teaching experience in graduate education. Hold or be eligible for occupational therapy licensure in the State of Indiana. Current certification with National Board for Certification in Occupational Therapy ( NBCOT ) Department Contact for Questions For more information on the position, please contact search committee chair, Dr. Sally Wasmuth, at swasmuth@iu.edu . Additional information on the Occupational Therapy Program and the School of Health & Human Sciences can be found at https://shhs.i ndianapolis.iu.edu/. Additional Qualifications Post-doctoral training Interdisciplinary, team science approach to research Experience in mentoring graduate students Demonstrated expertise and research experience in occupational therapy focused on sports-related practice areas including sports, athletic performance, and/or adult rehabilitation Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101676-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34478": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Medicine",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Medicine Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Medicine Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Medicine Specific Title Pulmonary Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medicine - Pulmonary Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary A postdoctoral position is immediately available in the Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Sleep, and Occupational Medicine in the Department of Medicine. A full-time postdoctoral fellow position is available to conduct research focused on the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying the development of lung diseases and lung cancer with inhaled exposures. The Sears’ federally funded laboratory focuses on the impact of DNA damage and repair on the development of lung cancers and in treatment response. Our overall goal is to determine the key mechanisms involved in early exposure-related lung cancer development which can be used to identify prognosis and/or targeted with therapeutics to prevent progression. We are seeking a highly motivated and curious post-doctoral candidate will be involved in both in vitro and in vivo research using animal models, primary, and immortalized cell culture models, molecular and cellular techniques, immunofluorescence, and histological analyses. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Qualifications and Experience: - Ph.D. or terminal degree in related field. - Experience with basic molecular/cellular biology techniques, including DNA / RNA /Protein isolation and analysis, histology, immunofluorescence/ immunohistochemistry, microscopy and quantitative and qualitative image analyses. -Strong organizational, analytical, communication, scientific recording and writing skills. This candidate must be able to work both independently and collaboratively. -Interest in lung cancer, inhalational exposures, or related areas of lung disease. Preferred Skills/Experience/Attributes: -Experience with primary cell culture and/or animal models. -Experience with research related to DNA damage, DNA damage response and pulmonary or cancer epithelial cell biology. Department Contact for Questions For further information contact: Angie Jones at arcannon@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02522-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Letter of Application Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34438": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please include (1) a letter of interest, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information for 3 references. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Specific Title Academic Hematopathologist Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at the Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) in conjunction with IU Health Medical Group ( IUHMG ), is seeking an academic hematopathologist to join its expanding faculty. The individual selected will be part of the Division of Hematopathology that provides expert diagnostic and consultative services to Indiana University Health (IU Health) which includes, Indiana University Hospital, Riley Hospital for Children, Methodist Hospital, IU Health West, IU Health North, IU Health Arnett, and numerous regional hospitals. The division also serves Eskenazi Health and the Richard L. Roudebush VA Medical Center. The successful candidate will join a collaborative group of ten hematopathologists with sign-out activities based in a central location, the IU Health Pathology Laboratory ( IUHPL ), in the downtown Indianapolis campus. Qualifications and level of experience determine the academic rank. Responsibilities: Provide diagnostic services including sign-out of tissue hematopathology, peripheral blood, and body fluids, as well as flow cytometry Pursue clinical and translational research. Actively participate in educational activities including ACGME -accredited and non-accredited trainees. Our Ideal Candidate: Is academically-oriented. Will take advantage of outstanding opportunities for clinical and translational research. Engage in academic teaching and mentoring of trainees. Interest or board certification/eligibility in molecular pathology is welcome but not required Interest in signing out coagulation testing is welcome but not required About the IU Health Pathology Laboratory and Department Financially-sound department, which is in a stage of growth and expansion. Stable environment with excellent retention practices and professional growth opportunities 150,000 square feet dedicated central laboratory building in downtown Indianapolis. First class facilities including flow cytometry and molecular pathology laboratories. IUHPL serves as the centralized anatomic and clinical pathology testing facility for numerous IU Health hospitals and affiliated clinics performing 15 million diagnostic tests annually. Environment that provides the ability to collaborate with a variety of subspecialized pathologists. Engaged in promoting innovation and excellence in the education of medical students, residents, and fellows. Strong departmental support for translational research Benefit Information: IUSM and IUHMG are committed to providing inclusive benefits for eligible employees and their families. To learn more, click here: IU – https://hr.iu.edu/benefits IUHMG – https://team.myiuhealth.org/benefits About Indianapolis: Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation About the IUSM : IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. https://policies.iu.edu/policies/ua-01-non-discrimination/ index.html Basic Qualifications Board certification in anatomic and/or clinical pathology, and board-certified or board-eligible in hematopathology Doctorate or Terminal degree is required. Has effective interpersonal skills and ability to work and communicate with a variety of individuals and groups. Department Contact for Questions Magdalena Czader, M.D., Ph.D. Virgil Moon Professor of Pathology Director, Indiana University Health Pathology Laboratories Vice Chair, Clinical Pathology Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine Indiana University School of Medicine mczader@iuhealth.org Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Please include (1) a letter of interest, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information for 3 references. Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02520-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34437": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please include (1) a letter of interest, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information for 3 references. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Professor of Clinical Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Specific Title Transfusion Medicine Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at the Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) in conjunction with IU Health Medical Group ( IUHMG ), is seeking an academic pathologist trained in Transfusion Medicine to join its expanding faculty. The individual selected will provide expert diagnostic and consultative services to Indiana University Health (IU Health) which includes Methodist Hospital, Indiana University Hospital, Riley Hospital for Children, IU Health West, IU Health North, IU Health Arnett, and a number of smaller hospitals. Board certification in CP or AP/CP, as well as board certification or eligibility in Blood Banking/Transfusion Medicine are required. The academic rank is determined by qualifications and level of experience. Responsibilities: · Provide transfusion medicine and apheresis service · Participate in coagulation consultation service · Actively participate in educational activities including ACGME -accredited trainees · Opportunity for Cellular Therapy Processing Facility Directorship Our Ideal Candidate: · Is academically-oriented · Will take advantage of outstanding opportunities for translational and collaborative research Why Join Us: · Collaborative opportunities with a multidisciplinary team in a nationally recognized academic        medical center. · Access to cutting-edge diagnostic technologies and robust research infrastructure. · Competitive compensation and benefits package. · Opportunity to shape the future of Transfusion Medicine through leadership and innovation. · Financially-sound department, which is in a stage of growth and expansion. · Stable environment with excellent retention practices and professional growth opportunities. · 150,000-square foot dedicated central laboratory building in downtown Indianapolis · New 900-bed IUH Downtown Hospital opening in 2027 · First class facilities, including molecular pathology laboratories. · IUHPL serves as the centralized anatomic and clinical pathology testing facility for numerous IU Health hospitals and affiliated clinics performing 19 million diagnostic tests annually. · Engagement in promoting innovation and excellence in the education of medical students, residents, and fellows. · Strong departmental support for translational research. Benefit Information: IUSM and IUHMG are committed to providing inclusive benefits for eligible employees and their families. To learn more, click here: IU – https://hr.iu.edu/benefits IUHMG – https://team.myiuhealth.org/benefits About Indianapolis: Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation About the IUSM : IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. https://policies.iu.edu/policies/ua-01-non-discrimination/ index.html Basic Qualifications Doctorate or Terminal degree is required. The applicant must be certified by the American Board of Pathology in Anatomic Pathology and/or Clinical Pathology and be board-certified or eligible in Transfusion Medicine. Applicants must be qualified for licensure in the state of Indiana. Department Contact for Questions Magdalena Czader, M.D., Ph.D. Virgil Moon Professor of Pathology Director, Indiana University Health Pathology Laboratories Vice Chair, Clinical Pathology Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine Indiana University School of Medicine mczader@iuhealth.org Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Please include (1) a letter of interest, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information for 3 references. Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02519-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
