    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34416": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medicine - Nephrology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medicine at the Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking outstanding candidates for Postdoctoral Fellow positions in the Division of Nephrology. The Division’s research activities are led by world-renowned faculty members developing innovative protocols and driving the standard of care for diseases related to Nephrology. The search committee will begin evaluating applications as they are received and applications will continue to be reviewed until positions are filled. About the School and Department: The Indiana University School of Medicine https://medicine.iu.edu/ is the largest medical school in the US and is annually ranked among the top medical schools in the US by US News & World Report. Please visit https://medicine.iu.edu/internal-medicine for additional information about the Department of Medicine. About the Campus: To learn more about the Indiana University-Indianapolis campus, please visit: https://indianapolis.iu.edu/about/ About Indianapolis: Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications The candidate should have expertise in molecular and cell biology or related field. The candidate should be highly motivated and work both independently and within a team. The position requires excellent interpersonal and communication skills which will facilitate training and career development. Terminal degree required. Department Contact for Questions Please contact Tara Wilson at tarawils@iu.edu for any questions related to this posting. Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02487-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Letter of Application List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34413": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head &amp; Neck Surgery",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head &amp; Neck Surgery Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Specific Title Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Medicine Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery is seeking applicants for a clinical track faculty position in Head and Neck Oncologic Surgery. Candidates must have completed, or be expected to complete prior to the start of employment, an American Head and Neck Society ( AHNS )-accredited Head and Neck Surgical Oncology fellowship with specialized training in microvascular reconstruction. Candidates must also be board eligible or board certified in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to outstanding patient care, multidisciplinary collaboration, and teaching. The successful candidate will participate in a busy head and neck surgical oncology practice and contribute to the department’s missions of clinical excellence, education, and scholarship. Responsibilities include providing high-quality patient care within an established and robust multidisciplinary program focused on the management of patients with complex head and neck cancers. In addition to clinical duties, the successful candidate will teach surgical residents, medical students, and a head and neck surgical fellow within an institution committed to educational excellence. Participation in scholarly and academic activities is encouraged and supported. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community, and we seek candidates whose teaching, scholarly, and community engagement efforts contribute to a robust learning and working environment for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health and well-being throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctoral or terminal degree. Completion of an American Head and Neck Society ( AHNS )-accredited Head and Neck Surgical Oncology fellowship, including specialized training in microvascular reconstruction, is required. Candidates who are currently completing fellowship training and are expected to finish before the start of employment will also be considered. Must be board eligible or board certified in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Department Contact for Questions Sarah Maxwell smaxwell@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline 11/01/2026 Expected Start Date 12/01/2026 Posting Number IUSM-02521-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Letter of Reference 4 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34412": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Medical &amp; Molecular Genetics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Medical &amp; Molecular Genetics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Medical & Molecular Genetics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Medical & Molecular Genetics Specific Title Appointment Type Tenure Track Faculty Department IUSM - Medical & Molecular Genetics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics at the Indiana University School of Medicine is recruiting a Basic Scientist to serve as faculty. The Scientist will be appointed as Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (with tenure or on the tenure track depending upon qualifications). The successful candidate must have either a Ph.D. or M.D. degree and have expertise in molecular mechanisms of disease. The Department is particularly interested in candidates performing basic or translational research to address fundamental mechanisms of human genetic disease, develop functional genomic or therapeutic approaches, and/or use computational and bioinformatic approaches for genetic discovery and phenotypic delineation. The candidate must be able to mentor medical students, graduate students, residents and/or fellows in basic research. It is highly desirable for the holder of this position to have secured long term NIH funding, and to have demonstrated the ability to collaborate with others. The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics offers a highly collegial and interdisciplinary environment that spans clinical medicine and basic research. See https://medicine.iu.edu/genetics . Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Applicants should apply at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34237 Basic Qualifications Doctorate or Terminal degree is required. A PhD, MD, or equivalent and evidence of expertise in molecular mechanisms of disease. Department Contact for Questions Hunter Hartman johuhart@iu.edu Additional Qualifications The Department is particularly interested in candidates performing basic or translational research to address fundamental mechanisms of human genetic disease, develop functional genomic or therapeutic approaches, and/or use computational and bioinformatic approaches for genetic discovery and phenotypic delineation. Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02508-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34403": {
      "title": "Open Rank (Lecturer Track) in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems and Founding Program Director",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank (Lecturer Track) in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems and Founding Program Director Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank (Lecturer Track) in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems and Founding Program Director Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application Process Applications received by October 15, 2026, will receive full consideration. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Applicants should submit the following materials: A letter of application addressing: Interest in the position; Relevant teaching experience; Technical or professional expertise; Experience relevant to building and launching the proposed Program. A current curriculum vitae. Maximum of 3 pages summarizing qualifications and interest. A statement of teaching philosophy and practices. A two- to three-page statement describing the applicant's approach to: Program development and implementation; Laboratory-based technical education; Student recruitment, success, and retention; Employer, education-partner, and community engagement Names, titles, email addresses, and telephone numbers for three professional references. Applicants may also submit evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course evaluations, peer observations, teaching awards, or other appropriate documentation. Letters of recommendation will be requested only from candidates who advance to a later stage of the search. https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34359 Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank (Lecturer Track) in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems and Founding Program Director Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Columbus Columbus Location Columbus Position Summary Position Summary The Division of Behavioral, Health, and Life Sciences at Indiana University Columbus invites applications for a full-time, open-rank non-tenure-track faculty position in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems. The successful candidate will be rank of Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, or Teaching Professor, depending upon qualifications, experience, and the applicable Indiana University and academic-unit criteria. The successful candidate will also receive an initial administrative assignment as Founding Program Director for the proposed B.S. in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems . The position reports to the Head of the Division of Behavioral, Health and Life Sciences. The proposed 120-credit Bachelor of Science program is designed to prepare students to secure computing systems that monitor or control physical processes. It combines foundations in programming, data structures, operating systems, networks, databases, distributed systems, cloud computing, cryptography, and cybersecurity with required study of embedded and real-time systems, industrial controls, hardware and firmware security, wireless and Internet-of-Things security, operational-technology incident response, security architecture, verification and validation, and system resilience. The program is intended to serve advanced manufacturing, connected products, automotive and engine systems, industrial automation, robotics, material handling, utilities, healthcare technology, logistics, and other critical-infrastructure environments. Hands-on learning will include laboratory-intensive courses, CPS Security Studios, an isolated instructional cyber range, embedded devices, PLC / HMI equipment, industrial protocols, robotics or equivalent CPS platforms, and a two-semester applied capstone. The anticipated Spring 2027 assignment will focus primarily on program finalization, student recruitment, curriculum and course development, laboratory planning, partner engagement, and implementation. The initial semester is not expected to include a regular classroom teaching assignment. Following program launch, teaching will be a central responsibility of the position, accompanied by an administrative course release for program leadership. This is a 10-month academic appointment with a preferred start date of January 1, 2027. Summer program-development and launch responsibilities may be assigned and compensated separately through a summer stipend, subject to approval and available funding. The position is based at IU Columbus and requires regular on-campus presence for program leadership, recruitment, student engagement, laboratory development, and face-to-face classroom and laboratory instruction. Primary Responsibilities Program Development and Implementation Under the direction of the Division Head, the Founding Program Director will: Coordinate final development and implementation of the B.S. in Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems. Lead preparation and coordination of new-course proposals, CARMIn submissions, remonstrance materials, catalog information, and curricular documentation. Coordinate development of course schedules, prerequisite sequences, degree maps, and the staged course rotation. Develop and maintain program assessment processes, curriculum maps, direct-assessment plans, and documentation required for university and state review. Coordinate laboratory and cyber-range planning, including equipment selection, instructional environments, safety and authorized-use expectations, and integration of laboratory resources into the curriculum. Support implementation of the program’s Market Launch Plan, including student recruitment, information sessions, school and career-center outreach, campus visits, and laboratory demonstrations. Coordinate development of advising, transfer, credential, career-pathway, and student-success materials. Assist with future faculty searches and coordinate the contributions of program faculty under the direction of the Division Head. Coordinate program review, annual reporting, and continuing curriculum improvement. Participate, as assigned, in planning and stewardship of externally supported equipment and laboratory resources. Teaching and Student Success The faculty member will: Teach introductory and advanced undergraduate courses supporting the program. Develop and teach laboratory-centered, project-based, and applied learning experiences. Teach introductory programming or information-infrastructure coursework and courses in multiple areas of cybersecurity and cyber-physical systems. Advise and mentor students entering through first-year, transfer, adult, veteran, and technical-career pathways. Support student retention, progression, credential preparation, career exploration, and timely degree completion. Supervise or coordinate studios, applied projects, capstones, internships, or employer-informed learning experiences. Use appropriate in-person, blended, and Canvas-supported instructional practices while maintaining the program’s required face-to-face laboratory experiences. External and Institutional Engagement Under the direction of the Division Head, the faculty member will: Coordinate an employer and education advisory group for the program. Develop productive relationships with employers, school corporations, career and technical education programs, community colleges, community organizations, and professional associations. Support transfer-pathway development and continuing coordination with Ivy Tech Community College and Vincennes University. Coordinate employer-informed projects, guest speakers, facility visits, internships, co-ops, capstone opportunities, mentors, and career activities, subject to partner capacity. Represent the program at recruitment events, professional meetings, community forums, and appropriate university activities. Participate in faculty governance and service appropriate to the appointment. Professional Activity This is a teaching-focused appointment and does not require a traditional disciplinary research agenda. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain professional currency and engage in scholarship or professional activity that supports teaching, curriculum development, applied student learning, industry collaboration, and the mission of IU Columbus. Appointment Contingency This position, the Founding Program Director assignment, and the anticipated January 2027 start date are contingent upon final program approval, continued institutional authorization, and the availability of funding. About Indiana University Columbus Indiana University Columbus offers accessible Indiana University degree programs in Columbus, Indiana, and serves students and communities throughout south-central Indiana. The campus emphasizes student success, career-connected education, community engagement, transfer access, and collaboration with regional employers and education partners. The region provides an especially strong setting for a Cybersecurity for Cyber-Physical Systems program because of its concentration of advanced manufacturing, engineering, connected-product development, automation, logistics, healthcare technology, utilities, and other critical-infrastructure activity. Basic Qualifications Basic Qualifications Applicants must meet one of the following educational and professional preparation pathways: A terminal degree in computer science, cybersecurity, computer engineering, electrical engineering, informatics, information technology, systems engineering, or a closely related field by the appointment date; or A master’s degree in a relevant field together with substantial professional experience in cybersecurity, cyber-physical systems, operational technology, embedded systems, industrial automation, connected products, or a closely related area. Applicants must also demonstrate: Relevant academic or professional expertise in cybersecurity, cyber-physical systems, operational technology, embedded computing, critical-infrastructure protection, or a closely related field. The ability to teach introductory programming or information-infrastructure courses. The ability to teach in at least two of the following areas: Cybersecurity foundations; Computer networks and network security; Operational technology or ICS / SCADA security; Embedded systems and microcontrollers; Real-time operating systems; Industrial automation and controls; Hardware and firmware security; Wireless and IoT security; Secure software development; Incident response or digital forensics; Security architecture and critical-infrastructure resilience; Verification, validation, testing, quality, or functional safety; or Applied cryptography. Evidence of, or clear potential for, effective undergraduate teaching and student mentoring. The ability to develop applied, project-based, or laboratory-centered learning experiences. The ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, students, employers, educators, and community partners. The ability to perform the position’s responsibilities through regular on-campus presence at IU Columbus. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Kimdy Le, Division Head, kimle@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications Preference may be given to candidates who demonstrate one or more of the following: Successful teaching experience at the undergraduate level. Experience developing or teaching hands-on laboratories, studios, cyber ranges, or project-based technical courses. Experience with one or more of the following: PLC and HMI platforms; Industrial protocols and networks; Embedded devices and microcontrollers; FreeRTOS, Zephyr, or comparable real-time operating systems; Hardware and firmware security; Robotics or autonomous systems; Wireless and IoT environments; Automotive, material-handling, or connected-product networks; Hardware-in-the-loop or software-in-the-loop testing; Digital forensics or evidence-preservation environments. Professional experience in advanced manufacturing, automotive or connected products, engineering services, utilities, healthcare technology, logistics, material handling, building systems, or another critical-infrastructure sector. Familiarity with NIST operational-technology guidance, the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, the NICE Workforce Framework, ISA -95, ISA / IEC 62443, secure systems engineering, verification and validation, functional safety, business continuity, or system resilience. Experience with curriculum development, new-program implementation, academic assessment, accreditation or designation preparation, faculty coordination, or academic administration. Experience developing employer-sponsored projects, internships, co-ops, applied research, or capstone experiences. Experience recruiting and supporting students in a new or emerging technical program. Experience developing transfer pathways or working with community colleges, high schools, career centers, or career and technical education programs. Evidence of successful collaboration with employers, professional associations, schools, or community organizations. Relevant industry credentials, including Security+, Network+, Linux+, CCNA , GICSP , ISA / IEC 62443 certificates, CISSP , or comparable credentials. Experience teaching and mentoring students with varied educational, professional, and technical backgrounds. Salary and Rank Open Rank - Lecturer Track Special Instructions Application Process Applications received by October 15, 2026, will receive full consideration. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Applicants should submit the following materials: A letter of application addressing: Interest in the position; Relevant teaching experience; Technical or professional expertise; Experience relevant to building and launching the proposed Program. A current curriculum vitae. Maximum of 3 pages summarizing qualifications and interest. A statement of teaching philosophy and practices. A two- to three-page statement describing the applicant’s approach to: Program development and implementation; Laboratory-based technical education; Student recruitment, success, and retention; Employer, education-partner, and community engagement Names, titles, email addresses, and telephone numbers for three professional references. Applicants may also submit evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course evaluations, peer observations, teaching awards, or other appropriate documentation. Letters of recommendation will be requested only from candidates who advance to a later stage of the search. https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34359 For Best Consideration Date 10/15/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101691-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34392": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor of Elementary Literacy Education",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor of Elementary Literacy Education Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor of Elementary Literacy Education Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested candidates should submit: (a) letter of application, (b) curriculum vita, (c) one sample syllabus of a literacy course that the candidate taught, and (d) names, addresses, telephone numbers, and email addresses for at least three professional references. Applications received by December 1, 2026 are assured full consideration. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled. The starting date is August 1, 2027. Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor of Elementary Literacy Education Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Education Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Education at Indianapolis is seeking an exemplary Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor faculty member to contribute to our Urban Teacher Education program with teaching and research strengths in literacy education; a commitment to urban education and communities; and a readiness to partner with local schools to provide transformative teacher education. The School of Education at IU Indianapolis is an urban serving institution located in the heart of Indianapolis. Our School of Education has approximately 30 full time faculty all of whom are actively engaged in teaching, scholarship of teaching, and research focused on urban education. Many of our faculty have scholarship interests that focus on partnerships with urban communities and schools and critical perspectives in education. Our Teacher Education program is specifically designed to engage in partnerships with urban schools. For more information, visit our website at: www.education.indianapolis.iu.edu Duties: Design and implement literacy teacher education curriculum that grounds pre-service teachers in deep understanding of science of reading, evidenced-based culturally and contextually responsive pedagogy, human development, and urban communities within strong field-based experiences. Collaborate with faculty in the urban teacher education programs to develop mutually beneficial partnerships with local schools. Lead or assist in ongoing program development, innovation, and evaluation. Engage in the scholarship of teaching. Provide service to School and university committees, professional organizations, and the community. About the Campus IU Indianapolis is a dynamic urban research campus, located in the heart of the state’s capital and largest city. Positioned at the center of a thriving metropolitan region, the campus connects faculty and students with schools, community organizations, healthcare systems, businesses, cultural institutions, and industry partners—creating exceptional opportunities for teaching, research, collaboration, and community impact. Building on more than five decades of achievement in Indianapolis, IU Indianapolis is entering an exciting new era of growth and innovation. For faculty, the campus offers the opportunity to help shape the future of a growing urban research university while preparing students to make a meaningful difference in the communities and professions they serve. With Indianapolis as an extension of the classroom, IU Indianapolis offers the energy and opportunities of a major city alongside the resources and reach of Indiana University. Here, location creates possibility—for experiential learning, interdisciplinary scholarship, community-engaged research, and lasting professional connections. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications : Doctorate in literacy/reading education, curriculum studies, teacher education, urban education or multicultural education. Professional teaching experience in urban K-12 schools. Curricular and instructional expertise in science of reading, Culturally Relevant/Sustaining Pedagogy and other asset pedagogies. Capacity to teach the science of reading in undergraduate methods courses and graduate courses in masters programs. Demonstrated excellence in university teaching, including scholarship of teaching. Record of positive impact with youth in urban schools, universities, communities and/or families. Department Contact for Questions Direct questions about the position or program to Dr. Jarvais Jackson, Search Committee Chair, at jarjack@iu.edu . Individuals who require accommodations to participate in the application process should notify Patriece Roulette Landis at proulett@iu.edu or the Office of Civil Rights Compliance ( OCRC ) at ocrcindy@iu.edu with appropriate advance notice. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Special Instructions Interested candidates should submit: (a) letter of application, (b) curriculum vita, © one sample syllabus of a literacy course that the candidate taught, and (d) names, addresses, telephone numbers, and email addresses for at least three professional references. Applications received by December 1, 2026 are assured full consideration. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled. The starting date is August 1, 2027. For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101688-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Sample Syllabus List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34389": {
      "title": "Assistant Librarian",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Librarian Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Librarian Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please provide the names and contact information for three references. References will not be contacted without your prior knowledge. For best consideration, applications should be received by October 26, 2026. We encourage early career librarians to apply even if your experiences do not cover all preferred qualifications. We appreciate that librarians bring a wide variety of valuable life and work experiences. We are committed to working with our colleagues to develop the skills needed to support the mission of IUSM and the greater librarian community. Salary and rank depend on the candidate's qualifications and experience. The minimum salary is $60,000. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Librarian Specific Title Liaison Librarian Appointment Type Tenure Track Faculty Department IUSM - Ruth Lilly Medical Library Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Based in Indianapolis, IN, the Ruth Lilly Medical Library seeks a dynamic, collaborative, and talented Liaison Librarian. This position will plan, promote, and provide instruction and information services that support the needs of students, residents, faculty, researchers, and staff at the Indiana University School of Medicine (IU School of Medicine). In addition to serving as liaison to the Terre Haute IU School of Medicine campus, the successful candidate will have an opportunity to develop a specialty area that reflects their interests and expertise while advancing established priorities of the School of Medicine and the Library. This is a tenure-track faculty position reporting to the Associate Director for Public Services. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. We encourage early-career librarians to apply even if your experience does not cover all preferred qualifications. We appreciate that librarians bring a wide variety of valuable life and work experience. We are committed to working with our colleagues to develop the skills needed to support the mission of IU School of Medicine and the greater librarian community. IU School of Medicine is the nation’s largest medical school with nine campuses throughout Indiana. This school is one of the nation’s premier medical schools and is an innovative leader in medical education, research, and clinical care. IU School of Medicine is located in Indianapolis on the Indiana University Indianapolis campus. Indianapolis (fondly known as Indy) is the 16th largest city in the nation, the state capital, and has a cost of living below the national average. Indy has a walkable downtown, an ever-expanding network of bike trails, lots of independent bookstores and coffee shops, and multiple professional sports leagues. RESPONSIBILITIES Liaison Services · Serves as a liaison to the Terre Haute campus. The librarian will be based in Indianapolis but will develop strong relationships with Terre Haute faculty, staff, students, and campus leadership through pre-scheduled campus visits. · Serves as a liaison to departments/groups across our nine-campus statewide system · Contributes to university- and campus-wide initiatives to develop policies, services, and infrastructure to support research services through collaboration with IU School of Medicine faculty and staff. · Provides and contributes to curriculum, course development, training, and workshops for IU School of Medicine students, faculty, and staff. · Provides reference and expert searching services to support research, clinical practice, and medical education. · Conducts literature searches and supports systematic reviews. This may include consultation and instruction related to systematic reviews, scoping reviews, and other forms of evidence synthesis. Collaboration · Participates in a team approach to providing prompt and effective consultation, instructional services, and reference expertise to students, faculty, and staff. · Cultivates partnerships and collaborates with faculty on projects including teaching, grants, manuscripts, papers, or poster presentations. · Contributes to the overall development and innovation of the library and the profession by participating in library, campus, university, state, regional or national meetings, committees, workgroups, and task forces or by representing the library. · Develops a full network of contacts within assigned liaison areas (departments/units, institutes, regional IU School of Medicine campuses, and centers) and anticipates and meets their information needs. · Identifies opportunities for library partnerships within IU School of Medicine and Indiana University at large. · Works with others to develop and revise programs and services to respond to changing information needs. Scholarly/Professional · Maintains current knowledge of the trends and issues in biomedical research, higher education, scholarly communication, artificial intelligence, research practice, and health care. · Pursues an active and ongoing career plan in the areas of job performance, professional development/research, and service. Standards are outlined in the Indiana University – Indianapolis Promotion & Tenure Guidelines: https://provost.indianapolis.iu.edu/Faculty-Affairs-Resources/PT-Process-Timelines · Designs and implements a plan for continuing education, continuing professional development and actively contributes to the development of health sciences librarianship through scholarly and service activities. · Contributes to departmental projects and initiatives as needed to support organizational goals. Participates in other projects and initiatives as assigned. Basic Qualifications Required: · ALA -accredited Master’s degree or an equivalent advanced degree in a relevant field at the time of hire. · Experience or training in delivering instruction at the college/university level · Ability to work in a collaborative work environment and maintain positive professional working relationships with faculty members, supervisor, and peers · Demonstrated ability to design accessible written learning materials, and clarify complex research requests in consultations. · Ability to travel to IU School of Medicine regional campuses to deliver in-person instruction as required. · Excellent communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills. Department Contact for Questions Angi Hill Business Manager angihill@iu.edu 317-274-6468 Additional Qualifications Preferred: · Two years of professional experience in a health or sciences library or equivalent experience (e.g., graduate internships or other equivalent experience) · Ability to maintain knowledge on a broad range of biomedical and healthcare research topics · Record of scholarship, teaching, and/or active membership in a professional organization · Experience providing reference services · Experience with promoting or developing emerging technology services · Experience teaching evidence-based medicine concepts · Experience with conducting expert searching and/or systematic reviews. Special Instructions Please provide the names and contact information for three references. References will not be contacted without your prior knowledge. For best consideration, applications should be received by October 26, 2026. We encourage early career librarians to apply even if your experiences do not cover all preferred qualifications. We appreciate that librarians bring a wide variety of valuable life and work experiences. We are committed to working with our colleagues to develop the skills needed to support the mission of IUSM and the greater librarian community. Salary and rank depend on the candidate’s qualifications and experience. The minimum salary is $60,000. Priority Application Review Deadline 10/26/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IUSM-02511-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34375": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Radiology, Pediatric Radiology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Radiology, Pediatric Radiology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Radiology, Pediatric Radiology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Radiology, Pediatric Radiology Specific Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Radiology & Imaging Sciences Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Radiology & Imaging Sciences Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Pediatric Radiologist The Department of Radiology & Imaging Sciences at Indiana University School of Medicine in partnership with Indiana University Health Medical Group is seeking a full-time fellowship trained Pediatric Radiologist to join its pediatric radiology division for a clinical track faculty position to provide care of pediatric patients at Riley Hospital for Children at IU Health in Indianapolis and across the IU Health system and region. Candidates must be certified (or eligible) in Diagnostic Radiology by the American Board of Radiology or its equivalent and must have passed or be eligible for the CAQ examination in Pediatric Radiology. Willingness to join one or more of our subspecialist groups (fetal, cardiac, musculoskeletal, or neuroradiologists) is desired but not required. Benefits include a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits including medical, dental, CME , paid ACR membership, a business expense account, and paid malpractice insurance. Indiana University Health Medical Group is a multidisciplinary practice affiliated with Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ). The Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences includes approximately 180 clinical faculty, 52 residents, 10 physicists, and 30 research faculty. Riley Hospital for Children is a free-standing tertiary referral center and level one trauma center serving the entire state of Indiana. The Pediatric Division interprets approximately 180,000 examinations annually. Riley Hospital for Children is located in downtown Indianapolis but plays a central role in providing care for children across the state and is one of the top-rated children’s hospitals in the country. Numerous pediatric subspecialties at Riley Hospital for Children are consistently among the highest-rated nationally. The successful applicant would be joining a large Pediatric Radiology group which includes dedicated pediatric subspecialists such interventional radiologists, neuroradiologists, fetal imagers, cardiac imagers, musculoskeletal imagers, and emergency imagers (which staff all overnight hours). IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. Interested candidates please complete an application and submit a letter of interest, a current CV in PDF format, and contact information for 3 references. Basic Qualifications Candidates must be certified (or eligible) in Diagnostic Radiology by the American Board of Radiology or its equivalent and must have passed or be eligible for the CAQ examination in Pediatric Radiology. Doctorate or Terminal Degree Required Department Contact for Questions Carla Ewing, Associate Director, HR IUSM , Radiology & Imaging Sciences carewing@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02518-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34368": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Research Professor of Surgery – Surgical Oncology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Research Professor of Surgery – Surgical Oncology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Research Professor of Surgery – Surgical Oncology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Research Professor of Surgery – Surgical Oncology Specific Title Appointment Type Scientist Track Faculty Department IUSM - Surgery - Surgical Oncology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Medicine’s Department of Surgery, Division of Surgical Oncology, is seeking an Assistant Research Professor to join the lab of Dr. C. Max Schmidt. This faculty position will work on various projects to study mechanisms of pancreatic cancer initiation and progression, assist with ongoing projects and lab duties including assisting with running the tissue bank, and perform services that are necessary to implement the education programs and academic objectives of the Department and the Division of Surgical Oncology. This is a full-time position and salary will be commensurate with past experience. Department of Surgery – As one of the largest departments in the country and part of the only academic medical center in the state, the Department of Surgery at Indiana University School of Medicine cares for some of the highest volumes of surgical patients in the country with outstanding outcomes. The department is an international leader in surgical education, health services and outcomes, and quality improvement research. Our nationally recognized research centers are rapidly expanding, particularly in clinical and outcomes research, while our surgical education program continues to set the standard for innovation. With a new $6 billion campus to open in 2027, the Department of Surgery continues to have a tremendous impact on the Hoosier state and around the world. Indiana University School of Medicine – IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. IU Health – IU Health is Indiana’s most comprehensive healthcare system and the only academic center in the state, covering 7 million residents . Our unique integration with the Indiana University School of Medicine, one of the nation’s leading medical schools, provides physicians opportunities in research and innovation. Based in Indianapolis, Indiana, with dozens of facilities statewide, IU Health is a regional leader in providing the right healthcare when and where it is needed. IU Health is currently building a new $6B campus consisting of a new 900 bed primary academic hospital and multiple clinic and support buildings, while helping the surrounding community to thrive. Indianapolis – Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a varied population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation Basic Qualifications PhD and at least one year of postdoctoral research experience Department Contact for Questions Meghan Hutchinson Human Resources Business Partner Department of Surgery mjhutchi@iu.edu Or C. Max Schmidt, MD, PhD Professor of Surgery Professor of Biochemistry, Molecular Biology & Pharmacology maxschmi@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02517-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34362": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Pediatrics Specific Title Neonatologist Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Division of Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking to add a clinical track faculty physician to provide patient care and serve as a clinician educator. Expert neonatologists at Riley Children’s Health deliver specialized, evidence-based care to neonates in need of lifesaving choices. In many cases, our care begins prenatally and continues through the transition to home with comprehensive, multidisciplinary follow-up. Clinically, this role is expected to perform twenty weeks of clinical service at Riley Hospital NICUs or a local level 3 community NICU location. On an annual basis, call responsibilities will consist of twenty-four in-house calls, eight weekend rounding shifts, and moonlighting options. As an educator, there are numerous activities to advance programs within the department at both the student and resident level across all campuses. Faculty are involved in supervision and instruction of residents and medical students on clinical services. We also encourage participation in various didactic programs, as well as activities in the skills lab and simulation center. There are numerous activities to advance our research programs within the department and we anticipate this individual to actively participate and facilitate research activities either through direct investigator lead research projects or via referral of patients to ongoing clinical research projects. The Department of Pediatrics at IUSM /Riley Hospital for Children is a 398-member faculty that is consistently within the top 10 pediatric departments in NIH funded research in the United States and has 19 Fellowship programs. There is a new large pediatric clinical research center, a new pediatric outpatient center dedicated to general pediatrics and neurodevelopment related divisions in pediatrics, genetics, and psychiatry. There are also State-of-the-art laboratories in a dedicated pediatric research building, a clinical research center, a well-functioning biobank, six T32 fellow training grants and one K12s that support research in the Department. Our nationally recognized Division of Children’s Health Research is available for collaboration and as a resource for combined fellowships with the pediatric hospitalist program. The Department also has research relationships with the Regenstrief Institute in Health Informatics. Pediatric Radiology and Pediatric Neuroradiology are strong research partners. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree required. Completion of pediatric residency and Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine fellowship programs. Must be Board Certified/Eligible in Pediatrics & Neonatology. New graduates and experienced providers are encouraged to apply. Department Contact for Questions Jacqueline Boese jhboese@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02516-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? (Open Ended Question) * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34341": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Appointee",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Post Doctoral Appointee Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Post Doctoral Appointee Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Post Doctoral Appointee Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Microbiology & Immunology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Position Title: Postdoctoral Fellow in Peptide Chemistry and Cancer Immunotherapy The Peptide Laboratory at the Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking an outstanding and highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join a multidisciplinary research program focused on the discovery and translation of next-generation peptide-based cancer immunotherapies. The laboratory has pioneered B-cell epitope peptide vaccines targeting immune checkpoint molecules and oncogenic growth factor receptors and is advancing novel multivalent and multispecific vaccine platforms toward clinical translation. The successful candidate will lead innovative projects integrating peptide chemistry, structural immunology, cancer biology, and translational immunotherapy. This position offers the opportunity to work in a highly collaborative environment with access to state-of-the-art chemistry, proteomics, immunology, and animal research cores while contributing to projects with strong publication, patent, and clinical translation potential. Research Focus Current research programs include: Therapeutic peptide vaccines targeting PD-1, CTLA -4, TIGIT , IL1RAP, HER -2/ HER -3, VEGF , and other immune and tumor-associated targets Development of multispecific and multivalent peptide vaccine platforms Peptide conjugation using click chemistry and advanced linker technologies Structural design and optimization of conformational B-cell epitopes Combination immunotherapy with chemotherapy, targeted therapies, and immune checkpoint modulation Translation of peptide vaccines from preclinical models to Phase I clinical trials Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Synthesize therapeutic peptide vaccine candidates using Fmoc solid-phase peptide synthesis ( SPPS ). Develop and optimize peptide conjugation strategies, including CuAAC, SPAAC , thiol-maleimide coupling, PEGylation, and multivalent scaffold assembly. Purify and characterize peptides using analytical and preparative HPLC , LC-MS, and MALDI - TOF mass spectrometry. Execute studies evaluating vaccine immunogenicity and antitumor efficacy in preclinical models. Collaborate with multidisciplinary teams in chemistry, immunology, oncology, and bioinformatics. Analyze and interpret experimental data and maintain detailed laboratory records. Prepare manuscripts for publication in high-impact journals. Contribute to NIH grant applications, progress reports, and intellectual property development. Mentor graduate students, technicians, and undergraduate trainees. Present research findings at national and international scientific meetings. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation Faculty Affairs and Professional Development | IU School of Medicine Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Chemistry, Medicinal Chemistry, Chemical Biology, Biochemistry, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Immunology, or a closely related field. Demonstrated expertise in peptide synthesis and purification. Strong publication record in peer-reviewed scientific journals. Experience designing and troubleshooting independent research projects. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to collaborative research. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in one or more of the following: Automated and manual Fmoc solid-phase peptide synthesis Peptide cyclization and constrained peptide design Click chemistry (CuAAC and SPAAC ) Bioconjugation chemistry and linker design Reverse-phase HPLC method development LC-MS and MALDI - TOF characterization Cancer immunology Tumor immunology Vaccine development Immune checkpoint biology Mouse models of cancer Flow cytometry ELISA and antibody characterization Cell culture and molecular biology techniques Department Contact for Questions Gerri Herron HR Coordinator geherron@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date 11/02/2026 Posting Number IUSM-02499-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34332": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Tourism, Conventions, and Event Management – School of Health & Human Sciences Location Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University Indianapolis invites applications for a 10-month tenure-track Assistant Professor in Tourism, Hospitality, or Event Management to support its expanding programs in tourism, events, hospitality, and sport management. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2026. Located in the heart of downtown Indianapolis, IU Indianapolis offers a distinctive urban research environment in one of the nation’s premier destinations for conventions, mega-events, and sports tourism. Indianapolis is internationally recognized as the “Sports Capital of the World,” home to the NCAA headquarters, major professional sports franchises, global sporting events, and a thriving sport-tech ecosystem. The city also features one of the most highly regarded convention centers in the United States—the Indiana Convention Center—positioning Indianapolis as a leading convention and mega-event destination. This setting provides unparalleled opportunities for industry collaboration, applied research, and experiential learning partnerships across tourism, hospitality, event management, and sport industries. Position Overview The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate instruction in areas such as: Tourism and destination management Convention and event management Hotel operations and revenue management Hospitality leadership and strategy Sport and event tourism Tourism and hospitality analytics Sustainable and resilient destinations Experience design We welcome applicants committed to addressing complex challenges in tourism systems, destination competitiveness, event strategy, hospitality innovation, sustainability, technology integration, and data-informed decision-making. Research and Collaboration Opportunities We seek scholars who create or employ innovative qualitative, quantitative, or computational methods to examine issues including: Convention and mega-event impact Sport tourism and event-driven development Urban destination strategy and competitiveness Hospitality performance and revenue optimization Tourism analytics and predictive modeling Sustainable tourism and community engagement Technology, AI, and smart destinations Indianapolis provides a living laboratory for tourism and event research. Faculty have strong potential to collaborate with: The Indiana Convention Center and Visit Indy NCAA and national governing sport bodies Professional sport franchises and event organizers Major recurring events (e.g., large-scale sporting championships, conventions, and festivals) Hospitality leaders, venue operators, and destination management organizations Responsibilities Teach undergraduate courses in tourism, hospitality, event management, sport tourism, or related domains. Establish and sustain a productive research agenda with potential for external funding. Develop applied partnerships with tourism, hospitality, convention, and sport industry stakeholders. Contribute to interdisciplinary initiatives and community engagement. Mentor undergraduate students. Provide service at the departmental, school, and university levels. Support learners through effective, evidence-based teaching practices. Duties 50% Research 40% Teaching (2 courses/fall and spring semesters) 10% Service About IU Indianapolis IU Indianapolis is Indiana’s premier urban public research university and holds both Community Engagement and R1 Carnegie Classifications. The campus serves more than 22,000 students across 15 schools and is making major investments in research infrastructure, including: The Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute The Institute for Human Health and Wellbeing IU LAB at 16 Tech, supported by a $138 million Lilly Endowment grant The School of Health & Human Sciences prepares graduates to lead in complex global industries and seeks scholars committed to impactful research, innovative teaching, and meaningful engagement with Indianapolis and beyond. Basic Qualifications Ph.D. in a related discipline, including but not limited to: hospitality, tourism, leisure studies, event management, sport tourism, or others Demonstrated potential for success in research as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications and presentations. Potential to secure independent external funding with collaborative research in the health sciences and related fields. Evolving research program aligned with or complementary to the IU Indianapolis School of Health & Human Sciences. Teach undergraduate courses in tourism, hospitality, event management, sport tourism, or related domains. Develop applied partnerships with tourism, hospitality, convention, and sport industry stakeholders. Provide service at the departmental, school, and university levels. Department Contact for Questions For more information on the position, don’t hesitate to get in touch with the search committee chair, Dr. David Pierce, at dpierce3@iu.edu . Additional information on the School of Health & Human Sciences, as well as the Department of Tourism, Event and Sport Management, is available at: https://shhs.indianapolis.iu.edu/index.html . Additional Qualifications Post-doctoral training or equivalent field/industry experience College teaching experience Potential for collaborative and/or interdisciplinary research across SHHS and the IU Indianapolis campus History of independent external funding with collaborative research with Industry and the private sectors Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 08/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101508-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34326": {
      "title": "Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Liberal Arts Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Ray Bradbury Center in the School of Liberal Arts at IU Indianapolis invites applicants for a Visiting Assistant Clinical Professor position in the American Studies Program in the Department of Interdisciplinary Studies. The person in this position will bring expertise as an experienced Project Archivist to lead the arrangement, description, preservation, and public-access preparation of approximately 186 linear feet of unprocessed manuscript materials from the Ray Bradbury Collection. The Center holds over 150,000 manuscript pages, four decades of correspondence, Bradbury’s working library of nearly 4,000 volumes, and the contents of his home office. This role requires prior archival processing experience, knowledge of archival metadata standards, and hands-on ArchivesSpace (or comparable) experience. Bradbury’s filing practices were often associative and inconsistent, so the archivist must exercise sound professional judgment when original order is unclear. The Project Archivist reports to the Center’s Director and collaborates with IU Libraries, University Collections, and the IU Indianapolis Arts and Humanities Institute. Established in 2007, the Ray Bradbury Center is the only public archive dedicated exclusively to Ray Bradbury (1920–2012), preserving manuscripts, correspondence, artwork, AV materials, and memorabilia documenting his creative and professional life. IU Indianapolis is opening a purpose-built reference library, reading room, and archival storage facility for the Center in October 2026. This position offers the opportunity to establish lasting intellectual and physical control over a major American literary archive and make it available for scholarly and public use. The initial appointment for this grant-funded visiting position is December 1, 2026, through August 31, 2028. This position requires regular on-campus presence. As the state’s premier urban research institution, IU Indianapolis is a dynamic campus of over 20,000 students and has been recognized for excellence in civic engagement and community partnerships. IU Indianapolis is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose scholarly efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications · Master’s degree from an ALA -accredited LIS program, or equivalent graduate degree with substantial archival training. · Professional experience arranging, processing, and describing archival or manuscript collections, including applying archival metadata/descriptive standards. · Hands-on experience with ArchivesSpace or a comparable collection management system; working knowledge of DACS and EAD . · Experience with structured data, spreadsheets, CSV files, or metadata migration workflows. · Demonstrated understanding of provenance, arrangement, description, preservation, and access; ability to make defensible arrangement decisions independently. · Strong written communication, organizational, and project-management skills; ability to handle fragile and sensitive materials appropriately. Preferred Qualifications · Multiple years processing large or complex personal papers, literary manuscripts, or 20th-century cultural collections. · Experience with complex, multi-level finding aids; advanced ArchivesSpace and CSV /batch-import experience; legacy metadata normalization. · Experience with restricted records, authority control, and mixed-format collections (photographs, art, AV, oversize). · Familiarity with 20th-century American literature, science fiction, or publishing history; experience on grant-funded processing projects. Department Contact for Questions Jason Aukerman (jaukerma@iu.edu) Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/15/2026 Expected Start Date 12/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101670-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34324": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head &amp; Neck Surgery",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head &amp; Neck Surgery Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor or above of Clinical Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Specific Title Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery in Indianapolis, Indiana, is seeking full-time faculty physicians to join its comprehensive and growing academic department. Successful candidates will participate in a busy general otolaryngology practice while contributing to the department’s missions of clinical excellence, education, and scholarship. Responsibilities include providing high-quality patient care, teaching residents, medical students, and fellows, and engaging in scholarly and academic activities. Academic rank and compensation will be commensurate with qualifications and experience IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or terminal degree; Board-eligibility or certification in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery; Candidate must be trained in all aspects of otolaryngology-head and neck surgery. Department Contact for Questions Sarah Maxwell smaxwell@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline 01/01/2027 Expected Start Date 09/01/2027 Posting Number IUSM-02515-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents List Of References Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Letter of Reference 4 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
