    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34750": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science,",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Biostatistics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow interested in developing and applying novel statistical methodologies for quantitative neuroimaging and multiview data integration, as related to Alzheimer’s Disease and the impact of substance exposure on neurodevelopment. The ideal individual will have a strong history in coding in R, Matlab, and/or Python, and statistical skills in any of (but not limited to) the following areas, including high-dimensional data analysis, time series, functional data, Bayesian statistics, random object analysis, and optimal transport. Experience with neuroimaging data processing and analysis is a plus. The fellows will work with an interdisciplinary team spanning multiple departments and institutes with a common goal of creating and evaluating novel statistical algorithms in healthcare and neuroscience. The fellow will work on statistical methodologies across a broad range of modalities and measurements including: • Resting-state and task-based functional MRI • Anatomical/structural MRI • Diffusion MRI • Multi-omics data • Clinical/behavioral outcomes The Indianapolis Campus is the focal point of health professions education at Indiana University, and the School of Medicine is the country’s second largest allopathic medical school. Indianapolis consistently ranks high nationally on many of the “best places to live” lists and has an economy that is growing in the life sciences arena. In addition, it has always been one of the cities with the lowest cost of living. Carmel, Indy’s northern neighbor, was recently named as the best mid-sized city in the country. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation The search will continue until the positions are filled. Basic Qualifications Completed PhD in biostatistics, statistics, or a related discipline Prior neuroimaging experience Strong skills in statistical methodology development and implementation Strong programming skills, experience with imaging software is a plus Department Contact for Questions Yi Zhao, Associate Professor yz125@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02544-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Writing Sample Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34747": {
      "title": "Clinical Laboratory Director",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Laboratory Director Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Laboratory Director Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Laboratory Director Specific Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Clinical Medical & Molecular Genetics Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Medical & Molecular Genetics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics of the Indiana University School of Medicine is recruiting a Board-certified/Board Eligible M.D., or MD/PhD, or PhD for a Clinical Laboratory Director role in the Division of Diagnostic Genomics and Therapeutics. We seek an outstanding physicians with expertise in Clinical Molecular Genetics and/or Cytogenetics who is interested in combining leadership in genomic laboratory medicine with clinical practice and/or funded research. The successful candidate will devote a substantial portion of their effort to medical and operational leadership within the IU Genetic Testing Laboratories, with the remainder of their effort dedicated to patient care or, depending on the candidate’s interests and qualifications, externally funded research. In addition, the individual will participate in our ACGME -accredited Laboratory Genetics and Genomics fellowship training program and participate in training residents and graduate students as part of the departmental teaching mission. The academic appointment, salary and benefit package is commensurate with qualifications. Certification (or eligibility) by the ABMGG in both Clinical Molecular Genetics and Cytogenetics, or Laboratory Genetics and Genomics, is required. The Division of Diagnostic Genomics and Therapeutics includes five CLIA -certified and CAP -accredited clinical laboratories that together comprise the IU Genetic Testing Laboratories: Molecular Genetics, Cytogenetics, Pharmacogenomics, Biochemical Genetics and Diagnostic Genomics, which performs high-throughput Next-Generation Sequencing. The laboratories provide comprehensive diagnostic services, including prenatal, constitutional, and oncology testing, supporting patient care throughout Indiana and beyond. The Medical Director will participate in the leadership and continued development of a busy and expanding diagnostic laboratory service. Responsibilities will include shared oversight of clinical genomic testing, interpretation and reporting, assay development and implementation, quality and regulatory activities, and collaboration with laboratory faculty, staff, clinicians, and other members of the healthcare team. The specific scope of laboratory leadership responsibilities will be tailored to the expertise and interests of the successful candidate. IU School of Medicine is the largest medical school in the United States. With nine campuses located throughout the state of Indiana, IUSM provides students and faculty with access to a large and varied patient population. Due to the strong collaborative partnerships between the school and statewide teaching hospitals and other clinical facilities, individuals seeking health care services from IUSM faculty receive the highest quality care that reflects advances in medical research. The Department of Medical and Molecular Genetics at IUSM was one of the first human genetics departments in the country and has a rich history of training geneticists and genetic counselors and providing genetic consultation and counseling services. The department offers a highly collegial and interdisciplinary environment that spans clinical medicine and basic research. See https://medicine.iu.edu/genetics . Join a vibrant and collaborative research community at the nation’s largest medical school . Apply today and help shape the future of translational genetics research. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the state of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation . Applicants should apply at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/32605 Basic Qualifications Doctorate or Terminal degree required. ABMGG board certification or board eligibility is required. Department Contact for Questions Hunter Hartman – johuhart@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02393-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34694": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Associate or Full Professor Faculty Position in Nursing",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Tenured/Tenure-Track Associate or Full Professor Faculty Position in Nursing Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Tenured/Tenure-Track Associate or Full Professor Faculty Position in Nursing Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Tenured/Tenure-Track Associate or Full Professor Faculty Position in Nursing Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Indianapolis School of Nursing Location Bloomington Position Summary Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for a tenured/tenure-track associate or full professor in the School of Nursing. We seek candidates to lead research in the IU School of Nursing’s clinical research areas of excellence : prevention and health promotion, chronic condition management and quality of life, serious illness care, and community and health systems. We are interested in candidates whose research advances health and well-being across populations and settings. Examples of areas that align with our current priorities include aging, rural health, environmental health, and community-engaged research. We invite applicants who have a record of federal-level funding and nationally and/or internationally disseminated and peer reviewed scholarship. These 10‐month appointments would ideally start August 1, 2027 , although an earlier or later start date is negotiable. Applications received by December 30, 2026 will receive full consideration, and applications will be accepted until the positions are filled. Responsibilities: The Indiana University School of Nursing is a core school with campuses in Bloomington, Fort Wayne, and Indianapolis. All School of Nursing faculty are tenured through IU Indianapolis. This position is located on the campus of Indiana University, Bloomington ( IUB ) in Bloomington, Indiana , and successful candidates are expected to work on the IUB campus to allow full engagement with the department, school, campus, and community. Indiana University bases its tenure and promotion recommendations upon performance in three areas including research, teaching, and service and scholars are expected to substantially contribute to the overall academic excellence of their department by developing and/or maintaining a robust research portfolio including pursuing and obtaining major extramural research funding to conduct cutting-edge research both as a corresponding principal investigator and a collaborating investigator; teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students as designated by the associate dean; and actively providing service within the department, school and university alongside active engagement with the profession and/or the community. Expected Research Contributions The faculty member will be expected to: Lead (PI, MPI ) at least two R01 or equivalent federal grant submissions annually ( NIH , AHRQ , CDC , PCORI , NSF ), contributing directly to IUB’s target of 200% growth in large external grants, and/or: Serve as a PI on community-engaged or translational research that advances the IUB goal of accelerating translational research with societal impact in Indiana. Pursue large-scale, federally coordinated grants (e.g., P-series, U-series) in collaboration with IUB partner schools. Publish peer-reviewed work in high-impact journals in nursing science, public health, or health services research, advancing IUB’s citation and Scholarly Research Index metrics Build a research team inclusive of PhD students, postdoctoral fellows, and undergraduate honors students Draw on existing IUSON PhD/Masters pipeline for collaborative research activity, connecting BL-based research projects with IU Indianapolis-based graduate programs Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a PhD in Nursing (or a related field; must have an MSN if PhD is not in nursing), hold Indiana RN licensure, and be committed to excellence in scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Candidates for w/tenure positions must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment w/tenure, including extramurally funded research as a corresponding principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, established record of publications and scholarly work, and experience leading sizeable research initiatives. Application Procedures: Review of applications will begin December 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Application materials should include: Cover letter (maximum of 3 pages in length, summarizing your interest in the position and your qualifications) Current CV Research and Teaching Statement (4 pages maximum, briefly discuss your current and future research agenda and your experience teaching and mentoring students) OPTIONAL Contact information for three professional references, which will only be contacted for those candidates selected for a screening interview Applicants should apply at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34657 All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Dr. Andrea Tanner, search chairperson, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. About Indiana University Bloomington : Founded in 1820, Indiana University-Bloomington ( IUB ) is the flagship campus of Indiana University. Located in Bloomington, IN., IUB constantly strives to embody its mission to create, disseminate, preserve, and apply knowledge. It does so through its commitments to cutting-edge research, scholarship, arts, and creative activity; inspired and challenging undergraduate, graduate, professional, and lifelong educational programs; meaningful experiences outside the classroom; expansive global educational opportunities while fostering a culturally diverse campus environment; first-rate library and museum collections; and far-reaching economic development and impact in the state and region. IUB is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities ( AAU ) since 1909, is accredited by the Higher Learning Commission and is a member of the North Central Association. With over 47,000 undergraduate and graduate students, the campus has 16 degree-granting colleges and schools offering 300+ undergraduate majors, 300+ Master’s degrees, and 150+ doctoral and professional degree opportunities. Known for its Indiana limestone facades and green spaces throughout, IUB is widely considered one of the country’s most beautiful college campuses. The Indiana University School of Nursing ( IUSON ), established in 1914, is one of the nation’s premier schools of nursing and among the largest nursing schools in the United States. As a core school spanning the Indiana University campuses in Bloomington, Fort Wayne, and Indianapolis, IUSON serves a diverse community of students, faculty, researchers, and healthcare partners across the state. With a strong tradition of excellence in education, research, and practice, IUSON prepares the next generation of nurse leaders through comprehensive undergraduate and graduate programs. As part of Indiana University, a leading research-intensive institution, IUSON is committed to advancing health through innovative research, interdisciplinary collaboration, community engagement, and the preparation of nurses and nurse scientists who improve health outcomes locally, nationally, and globally. Basic Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a Ph.D. in nursing (or a related field; must have an M.S.N. if Ph.D. is not in nursing), hold Indiana R.N. licensure, and be committed to excellence in scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Candidates for w/tenure positions must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment w/tenure, including extramurally funded research as a corresponding principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, established record of publications and scholarly work, and experience leading sizeable research initiatives. Department Contact for Questions All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Dr. Andrea Tanner, search chairperson, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Commensurate with experience Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 12/30/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101738-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement: Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement: IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Statement of Research and Teaching Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34867": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor of Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Research Professor of Pathology &amp; Laboratory Medicine Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Research Professor of Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Additional Information: Application materials should include a complete CV and a cover letter addressing their experiences, current interests or activities, and future goals. Also, please include names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin in November 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The start date for the position is negotiable. Required documents include: 1. Curriculum Vitae 2. Letter of Application 3. List of References Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Research Professor of Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Specific Title Appointment Type Scientist Track Faculty Department IUSM - Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at Indiana University School of Medicine is looking to further expand its basic, translational, and clinical research programs, particularly in the area of neurodegenerative diseases. The department is recruiting for a non-tenure track faculty position with interests in investigating the molecular, cellular, and especially structural mechanisms involved in progression of neurodegenerative diseases, although outstanding candidates in other areas, like using human induced pluripotent cells (iPSCs) to understand neurodegenerative diseases, will also be considered. Responsibilities: Expected to establish and maintain an energetic, independent, and well-funded research program. Contribute to the educational, service, and/or clinical missions of the medical school. Our Ideal Candidate: Has a strong record of peer-reviewed publications. Has had successful post-doctoral grant support. Has experience and interests in research that will complement the Department’s strategic research focus areas (neurodegeneration and structural biology). Will develop an independent, and well-funded research program. Contributes to the educational, service, and/or clinical missions of the medical school. Department Highlights: Financially sound department, which is in a stage of growth and expansion. Thriving, collegial, and supportive research environment that is rich with core facilities, robust cross-disciplinary graduate training programs, and expertise in AD and related dementias Valuable Resources that include the Stark Neurosciences Research Institute, the Indiana Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center ( IADRC ), the mouse MODEL -AD program and the TREAT -AD Centers for Discovery of New Medicines Benefit Information: IUSM is committed to providing inclusive benefits for eligible employees and their families. to learn more, click here: https://hr.iu.edu/benefits IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: Relocation Resources | Faculty Affairs and Professional Development | IU School of Medicine Basic Qualifications Terminal degree or doctorate degree in neuroscience or closely related field and relevant post-doctoral experience required. Department Contact for Questions Ruben Vidal, M.S., Ph.D. Luella Mc Whirter Martin Professor in Alzheimer Disease Research Professor of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine Department of Pathology & Laboratory Medicine Indiana University School of Medicine Email: rvidal@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Additional Information: Application materials should include a complete CV and a cover letter addressing their experiences, current interests or activities, and future goals. Also, please include names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin in November 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The start date for the position is negotiable. Required documents include: 1. Curriculum Vitae 2. Letter of Application 3. List of References Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02550-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34863": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Nephrology Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Medicine - Nephrology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Division of Nephrology at Indiana University School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join a multidisciplinary research program investigating the biology of uromodulin ( UMOD ), thick ascending limb ( TAL ) epithelial cells, and their roles in kidney injury and kidney stone disease . Our laboratory studies fundamental mechanisms regulating UMOD biology, including its intracellular trafficking, processing, secretion, alternative splicing, and cellular functions during kidney injury and repair . A major focus of the laboratory is understanding how alterations in UMOD biology influence TAL cell function, mitochondrial metabolism, acute kidney injury ( AKI ), progression from AKI to chronic kidney disease, and nephrolithiasis. Current projects include: Defining the molecular mechanisms regulating UMOD trafficking, intracellular processing, and secretion in TAL cells. Investigating alternative splicing of UMOD , including injury-induced UMOD isoforms with distinct intracellular localization and functions. Determining how UMOD regulates mitochondrial metabolism and cellular adaptation to kidney injury . Investigating mechanisms linking UMOD to kidney stone formation and nephrolithiasis . Developing therapeutic approaches that manipulate UMOD biology, including splice-modifying strategies . Integrating molecular and cell biology with animal models, advanced imaging, spatial biology, and human kidney tissue studies. The successful candidate will work closely with investigators with expertise in UMOD biology, renal physiology, kidney injury, animal models, spatial biology, and advanced imaging. The fellow will have opportunities to develop an increasingly independent research program, present work at national and international meetings, publish high-impact studies, participate in grant development, and receive structured mentoring toward an independent scientific career. The position is particularly well suited for an investigator interested in the intersection of cell biology, epithelial trafficking, RNA biology, mitochondrial biology, renal physiology, and kidney disease . Research and Career Development Environment The fellow will join a collaborative kidney research environment at Indiana University School of Medicine with access to expertise and infrastructure in renal physiology, molecular and cellular biology, animal models of kidney disease, advanced microscopy, spatial transcriptomics, spatial proteomics, and translational human kidney research. An important objective of the position is career development toward scientific independence . The fellow will be encouraged to develop independent hypotheses within the broader research program, lead manuscripts, present at national and international meetings, participate in grant writing, and develop an independent scientific niche over the course of the fellowship. The Division’s research activities are led by world-renowned faculty members developing innovative protocols and driving the standard of care for diseases related to Nephrology. The search committee will begin evaluating applications as they are received and applications will continue to be reviewed until positions are filled. About the School and Department: The Indiana University School of Medicine https://medicine.iu.edu/ is the largest medical school in the US and is annually ranked among the top medical schools in the US by US News & World Report. Please visit https://medicine.iu.edu/internal-medicine for additional information about the Department of Medicine. About the Campus: To learn more about the Indiana University-Indianapolis campus, please visit: https://indianapolis.iu.edu/about/ About Indianapolis: Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications The candidate should have expertise in molecular and cell biology or related field. The candidate should be highly motivated and work both independently and within a team. The position requires excellent interpersonal and communication skills which will facilitate training and career development. Qualifications Terminal degree required. Applicants should have a PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in cell biology, molecular biology, physiology, biochemistry, nephrology, or a related biomedical field. Candidates should be highly motivated, scientifically curious, and able to work both independently and collaboratively. Experience in one or more of the following areas is desirable: Molecular and cellular biology Protein trafficking and intracellular organelle biology RNA biology, alternative splicing, or transcriptomics Mitochondrial biology and cellular metabolism Kidney physiology or epithelial transport Acute kidney injury or chronic kidney disease models Nephrolithiasis or mineral metabolism Mouse models and experimental surgery Confocal, multiplexed, or advanced fluorescence imaging Spatial transcriptomics, spatial proteomics, or quantitative image analysis Prior expertise in kidney biology or UMOD is not required . Candidates with strong training in fundamental cell biology, RNA biology, protein trafficking, metabolism, or related areas who wish to apply these approaches to kidney disease are encouraged to apply. Strong communication skills and the ability to collaborate within a multidisciplinary research environment are expected. Department Contact for Questions Please contact Tara Wilson at tarawils@iu.edu for any questions related to this posting. Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02539-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Letter of Application List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34856": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Graham and DeRyckere labs are seeking a full-time post-doctoral researcher for a translational project focused on the TAM family kinases as therapeutic targets in leukemia. Responsibilities will include experimental design and execution, data analysis and presentation, participation in lab meetings and conferences, and teaching and supervising students and trainees. This position is expected to comprise 75% lab research and data analysis, 15% reading and writing grants/manuscripts/presentations and 10% teaching/mentoring students and trainees. Research in the Graham and DeRyckere labs, which function as a single unit, focuses on the TAM -family tyrosine kinase receptors and their roles in cancer cells and the tumor immune microenvironment. Our goal is to develop new therapies for treatment of cancer in children and adults and we are actively engaged in development of novel drugs. In conjunction with this work, we also develop and apply next-generation model systems to advance our research and for precision medicine applications. Our lab is a fast-paced collegial research environment where trainees at all levels are supported and challenged to develop new skills and knowledge. Indiana University is a multi-campus public research institution, and a world leader in professional, medical, and technological education with an annual operating budget of $2.1 billion. IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States with a student body that includes over 43% women and 36% ethnic minorities. IUSM scientists and physicians were awarded over $217million in research funding from the National Institutes of Health ( NIH ) in 2021, setting a school record for research funding for the sixth straight year. Additionally, IUSM provides an interdisciplinary and interactive scientific environment with many multidisciplinary centers and state-of-the-art core facilities. Indianapolis is a diverse, centrally located city that lies within 3 driving hours of other major population centers such as Chicago, Cincinnati, and Louisville. It is 14th largest city in the United States and is growing and thriving economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation, which contribute over $44 billion annually to the local economy. Our dynamic downtown is a bustling commercial center, entertainment destination and residential neighborhood. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. For additional information on life in Indy, visit https://medicine.iu.edu/indianapolis. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications PhD or MD/PhD in a science related field Department Contact for Questions Deb DeRyckere debderyc@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date 12/01/2026 Posting Number IUSM-02540-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34855": {
      "title": "Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Posting Details Position Details Title Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Chair Faculty Position Specific Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Psychiatry Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Psychiatry Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) and the Indiana University Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center ( IUSCCC ) invite applications for Walther Center Endowed Chair for Supportive Oncology position. This open-rank faculty position is designed for an investigator who is ready to grow a high-impact, collaborative research program in an environment with substantial institutional commitment, endowed-chair support, and access to Indiana’s only National Cancer Institute-designated comprehensive cancer center. The Walther Center brings together research and clinical care to improve the cancer experience for patients and families. The successful candidate will join a growing team of scholars and clinicians working across palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, survivorship, symptom science, patient-reported outcomes, health services research, and related fields. Why this opportunity is compelling · Endowed support: The chair is one of five Walther-endowed positions created to recruit academic leaders and accelerate supportive oncology research and care. · Collaborative environment: Candidates will engage with investigators, clinicians, trainees, IU Health partners, and community collaborators across a highly interdisciplinary academic health system. · Flexible fit: Faculty rank, department home, and clinical effort, where applicable, will be aligned with the candidate’s discipline, expertise, and career goals. · Growth orientation: The center is building a long-term initiative with opportunities for new programs, team science, pragmatic and translational research, implementation, and dissemination. · Mission and impact: The role offers a platform to improve quality of life, care delivery, health equity, and outcomes for people with cancer and their loved ones. Research focus areas We welcome applicants from a wide range of disciplines whose scholarship advances supportive oncology across the cancer continuum and across the lifespan including children, adolescents/young adults, and adults. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: · Psycho-oncology, behavioral oncology, mental health, coping, distress, resilience, and caregiver/family support · Palliative care, symptom management, cancer pain, fatigue, cachexia, sleep, communication, decision-making, and serious illness care · Survivorship, cancer and aging, sexual health, quality of life, rehabilitation, return-to-work and functional outcomes · Integrative oncology, exercise, physical activity, lifestyle interventions, nutrition, music therapy, and mind-body interventions · Health services research, implementation science, dissemination, digital health, patient-reported outcomes, health equity, and community-engaged research Responsibilities · Maintain and expand an independent, peer-reviewed research program in supportive oncology or a closely related field. · Develop innovative, collaborative, and translational scholarship that contributes to the growth and national visibility of the Walther Center. · Pursue external funding and publish high-impact scholarly work commensurate with rank and discipline. · Participate in multidisciplinary and team-science initiatives across IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and community partners. · Mentor learners, staff, trainees, and/or junior colleagues, commensurate with career stage and appointment level. · Contribute to teaching, service, leadership, and community engagement activities consistent with the candidate’s expertise and faculty appointment. About the Walther Center and IU Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center The Walther Center for Supportive Oncology is a major initiative of IUSM and IUSCCC , supported by the Walther Cancer Foundation and developed in partnership with IU Health. The center integrates palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, symptom management, survivorship, research, and clinical care to improve the lives of people with cancer and their families. IUSCCC is Indiana’s only NCI -designated comprehensive cancer center and a member of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. Cancer center investigators work across basic, clinical, translational, population, and community-engaged research, with substantial institutional resources to support research development, collaboration, and scientific innovation. About IU School of Medicine and Indianapolis IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States, with campuses across Indiana and a mission to advance health through education, research, and patient-centered care. IU Indianapolis is located in a growing life sciences region with strong academic, health care, philanthropic, community, and industry partnerships. Indianapolis combines the amenities of a major city with an accessible, livable metropolitan region. Candidates will find professional sports, arts and culture, restaurants, parks, trails, festivals, and diverse neighborhoods, as well as nearby communities such as Carmel, Fishers, Noblesville, and Zionsville that are frequently recognized for quality of life. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications · PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant discipline. · A productive independent research program with peer-reviewed extramural funding or a strong funding trajectory appropriate to rank. · Scholarly productivity and an emerging, established, or nationally recognized reputation in supportive oncology or a related field. · Demonstrated commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration across clinical, research, educational, and/or community settings. · Experience mentoring learners, staff, research teams, or junior colleagues, commensurate with rank and career stage. · Commitment to advancing inclusive excellence, health equity, and patient- and family-centered cancer care through research, teaching, mentoring, service, or community engagement. Department Contact for Questions psychr@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Priority Application Review Deadline 11/23/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02502-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34854": {
      "title": "Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter describing research vision and leadership experience, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Posting Details Position Details Title Director of Research, Walther Center for Supportive Oncology, Endowed Position Specific Title Assistant/Associate/Professor of Psychiatry Appointment Type Open Department IUSM - Psychiatry Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) and the Indiana University Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center ( IUSCCC ) invite applications for a nationally recognized investigator to serve as Director of Research for the Walther Center for Supportive Oncology. This open-rank faculty position offers an exceptional opportunity to shape a growing, endowed, interdisciplinary research program at Indiana’s only National Cancer Institute-designated comprehensive cancer center. The Director of Research will help define the next generation of supportive oncology science – research that improves the cancer experience for patients and families from diagnosis through treatment, survivorship, serious illness, and end-of-life care. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative environment with substantial institutional commitment, endowed-chair support, access to a broad clinical enterprise, and opportunities to integrate research, clinical care, education, and community engagement. Why this opportunity is compelling · Endowed platform: The position is supported by one of five Walther-endowed chairs established to recruit academic leaders in supportive oncology. · Program-building scope: The Director will have a visible leadership role in expanding, integrating, and sustaining supportive oncology research across the cancer center, IUSM , IU Health, and community partners. · Integrated research and care environment: The Walther Center encompasses both research and clinical care, creating opportunities to embed discovery into real-world oncology practice. · Flexible academic home: The primary department and faculty rank will be aligned with the candidate’s expertise, credentials, and scholarly discipline. · Clinical flexibility: Opportunities for limited clinical practice may be available for appropriately trained candidates and tailored to expertise and interests. · Statewide and national impact: IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and community partners offer opportunities for team science, implementation, dissemination, and health equity-focused research across a wide variety of populations. Research focus areas We welcome investigators whose work advances supportive oncology across the cancer continuum and across the lifespan including children, adolescents/young adults, and adults. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: · Psycho-oncology, psychosocial and behavioral oncology, cancer-related distress, resilience, and mental health · Palliative care, symptom science, patient-reported outcomes, communication, decision-making, and serious illness care · Survivorship, cancer and aging, caregiver and family outcomes, sexual health, and quality of life · Integrative oncology, rehabilitation, physical activity, exercise and lifestyle interventions, nutrition, music therapy, and mind-body interventions · Implementation science, dissemination, health services research, digital health, health equity, rural health, and community-engaged research Leadership responsibilities · Set and advance a strategic research vision for the Walther Center for Supportive Oncology in partnership with center and cancer center leadership. · Build collaborative, cross-disciplinary research programs that leverage the strengths of IUSM , IUSCCC , IU Health, and affiliated clinical and community partners. · Grow a high-impact portfolio of peer-reviewed extramural funding, publications, clinical and translational research, and dissemination initiatives. · Mentor faculty, trainees, and research staff; support career development for emerging investigators in supportive oncology. · Strengthen research infrastructure, including team-science models, data resources, patient-reported outcomes, pragmatic trials, and learning health system approaches. · Represent the Walther Center locally and nationally through scholarly leadership, partnerships, and visibility in supportive oncology research communities. About the Walther Center and IU Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center The Walther Center for Supportive Oncology is a major initiative of IUSM and IUSCCC , supported through transformational investment from the Walther Cancer Foundation and developed in partnership with IU Health. The center brings together complementary strengths in palliative care, psycho-oncology, integrative medicine, rehabilitation, symptom management, and survivorship to improve care and outcomes for people with cancer and their families. IUSCCC is Indiana’s only NCI -designated comprehensive cancer center and a member of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. Its investigators conduct basic, clinical, translational, population, and community-engaged research with a mission to reduce cancer mortality and suffering through discovery, education, and patient-centered care. About IU School of Medicine and Indianapolis IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States, with a statewide academic and clinical footprint and a strong record of research funding, education, and partnership. IU Indianapolis is located in the heart of a growing life sciences region with major health care, pharmaceutical, biotechnology, and innovation partners. Indianapolis offers the advantages of a major metropolitan area – professional sports, arts and culture, restaurants, trails, parks, festivals, and an accessible international airport – with a cost of living and quality of life that support recruitment and retention. The region includes nationally recognized communities such as Carmel, Fishers, and Noblesville, along with a wide range of urban, suburban, and rural living options. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications · PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant discipline. · An established, independently funded research program with a sustained record of peer-reviewed extramural funding and scholarly productivity. · National or international recognition in supportive oncology or a closely related field. · Demonstrated success leading interdisciplinary research initiatives and collaborating across clinical, academic, and community settings. · Record of mentoring learners, junior faculty, and/or multidisciplinary research teams. · Commitment to advancing inclusive excellence, health equity, and patient- and family-centered cancer care through research, teaching, mentoring, service, or community engagement. Department Contact for Questions psychr@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter describing research vision and leadership experience, curriculum vitae, statement of current and future research interests, and names of references or referee contact information as requested by the search committee. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services. IUSM is committed to cultivating a welcoming campus community and seeks candidates whose research, teaching, mentoring, service, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. Priority Application Review Deadline 10/20/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02503-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34850": {
      "title": "Director of Research",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Director of Research Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Director of Research Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Director of Research Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Lilly Family School of Philanthropy Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University Lilly Family School of Philanthropy seeks an ambitious, collaborative, and externally oriented leader to serve as Director of Research . The Director will hold a Research Scientist/Research Professor appointment with open rank , commensurate with qualifications and experience, and will serve as a member of the School’s senior leadership team . The Director will lead the School’s research enterprise and help shape its next era of intellectual leadership, strategic growth, partnership, and impact. Working with an accomplished research team, faculty, School institutes and programs, Indiana University colleagues, and partners around the world, the Director will strengthen the School’s position as a global leader in research on philanthropy and generosity. The School has an exceptional foundation on which to build, including signature research endeavors such as Giving USA research, the Philanthropy Panel Study, and the Global Philanthropy Indices , along with a broad portfolio examining giving, volunteering, fundraising, philanthropic behavior, institutions, and civil society. The next Director will strengthen these assets while identifying important new questions, partnerships, resources, and opportunities for growth. Central to the role will be deepening existing relationships and developing new local, national, and global partnerships; connecting the School’s research with faculty and expertise across the field; and ensuring that research is not only rigorous and respected, but used and useful . Key Priorities Lead an Exceptional Research Enterprise Provide strategic and intellectual leadership for the School’s research portfolio and professional research team, fostering a culture of rigor, collaboration, innovation, and impact. Steward and strengthen signature research endeavors, including Giving USA research, the Philanthropy Panel Study, and the Global Philanthropy Indices , while identifying emerging questions and opportunities where the School can provide distinctive leadership. Encourage innovation in research methods, data, technology, and partnerships while maintaining the highest standards of research integrity and independence. As a member of the School’s senior leadership team, contribute to broader strategy, priority-setting, resource development, and institutional leadership. Build Partnerships and Drive Growth Make external partnership development a central component of the School’s research strategy. Deepen relationships with existing funders and research partners while developing new relationships with foundations, nonprofits, corporations, associations, government entities, universities, research institutions, and other partners locally, nationally, and globally. Build a strong pipeline of collaborative and externally supported research opportunities, including ambitious multi-year and multi-partner initiatives. Work closely with the School’s Advancement and Professional Development teams to cultivate funders and partners and create connections among research, philanthropic leadership, professional learning, and practice. Leverage all the School’s Talent and Resources Build strong partnerships with faculty and create opportunities for faculty expertise and the School’s professional research capabilities to reinforce one another. Collaborate with the Lake Institute on Faith & Giving, Women’s Philanthropy Institute, and Mays Family Institute on Diverse Philanthropy to advance complementary research, partnerships, dissemination, and engagement. Connect the School with expertise across field, creating and joining strategically beneficial interdisciplinary initiatives that draw upon the school’s distinctive strengths. Represent the School on campus and system wide research committees, liaison between university and faculty to promote and advance LFSOP faculty research. Illuminate Policy and a Changing Philanthropic Environment Advance the School’s emerging interest in research that illuminates policy possibilities and examines the consequences of policy change for philanthropy, nonprofits, donors, communities, and civil society. Use the School’s data, research capabilities, and interdisciplinary expertise to provide timely, credible, and nonpartisan evidence that informs public understanding and decision-making. More broadly, anticipate important changes affecting philanthropy and civil society and position the School to provide research leadership on the questions that will shape the field. Make Research Used and Useful Develop innovative approaches to translating and disseminating research so that it reaches and engages practitioners, donors, nonprofit leaders, policymakers, scholars, journalists, and communities. Experiment with new forms of engagement—including digital and interactive tools, convenings, executive briefings, visualization, media partnerships, AI-enabled approaches, and other emerging models. Create stronger pathways from research to insight to engagement to action , while developing ways to understand the reach and influence of the School’s research. Basic Qualifications Leadership Profile and Qualifications The successful Director will combine the strengths of a research leader, relationship builder, strategist, entrepreneur, and translator . They will bring intellectual curiosity and commitment to rigorous research together with an instinct for building relationships, developing people, mobilizing resources, and creating opportunities. The Director must be comfortable engaging researchers and faculty as well as philanthropic and nonprofit leaders, funders, university leadership, policymakers, and international partners. They will lead through collaboration and help connect people and ideas across institutional and disciplinary boundaries. The successful candidate is expected to hold an earned doctorate in a social science field or closely related discipline . An equivalent combination of distinguished professional experience, research leadership, and relevant academic or applied expertise may also be considered, consistent with Indiana University requirements for appointment to an open rank. Competitive candidates will bring: Significant leadership experience in research, philanthropy, higher education, public policy, nonprofits, a research institute, foundation, think tank, or related environment. Experience leading and developing a high-performing research team. A record of building substantial external partnerships and securing resources for research or strategic initiatives. Deep respect for methodological rigor, research integrity, and scholarly independence. Exceptional ability to build trust and collaborate with faculty, funders, practitioners, donors, institutional leaders, and partners across sectors. Superb communication skills and the ability to make sophisticated research relevant and accessible. An entrepreneurial and innovative orientation, combined with the judgment necessary to serve as a senior leader within a complex academic institution. A record of accomplishment appropriate for appointment as a Research Scientist/Research Professor at Indiana University. Department Contact for Questions Sara Konrath Search Committee Chair Associate Professor of Philanthropic Studies skonrath@iu.edu Additional Qualifications The Opportunity The Lilly Family School of Philanthropy offers a combination of assets found nowhere else: a school devoted exclusively to philanthropy; distinguished faculty and research professionals; signature research programs and distinctive data assets; respected institutes; deep relationships throughout the field; and the intellectual resources of Indiana University. The aspiration is not simply to produce more research. It is to identify the questions that matter, build the partnerships and resources needed to answer them, produce knowledge people trust, and ensure that research reaches and influences the people who can put it to use. As both the leader of the School’s research enterprise and a member of its senior leadership team, the Director will have an unusual opportunity to help shape the School’s broader future while extending its local, national, and global leadership in understanding philanthropy and generosity. Salary and Rank $175,000 - Open Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/29/2026 Expected Start Date 07/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101708-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Evidence of Teaching Ability Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 URL Publication 1 Publication 2 Writing Sample Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34847": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Indianapolis Campus",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Indianapolis Campus Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Indianapolis Campus Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Open positions are available for August 2027 start dates. Applications received by December 1, 2026, will be given priority. The documents below must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34815 . Letter of application/Cover Letter (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Statement of Research and Teaching/Personal Statement (5-page limit) outlining achievements and goals in teaching, research, and service e.g. https://academicaffairs.indianapolis.iu.edu/Faculty-Affairs/PromotionTenure/guidelines-and-standards Contact list with three references For additional information, please contact Dr. Wendy Trueblood Miller, Acting Dean, Executive Associate Dean for Research at wrtruebl@iu.edu or Dr. Julie Otte, Executive Associate Dean of Analytics at jlelam@iu.edu. Additional information is available at nursing.iu.edu , including details about our Areas of Research Excellence: https://nursing.iu.edu/research/areas-research-excellence/index.html Posting Details Position Details Title Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Indianapolis Campus Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Nursing Location Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Nursing ( IUSON ) seeks qualified applicants for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of assistant professor. We invite applicants who are poised to develop a record of nationally and/or internationally disseminated and peer reviewed scholarship. IUSON is offers a full range of academic programs from BSN to DNP and PhD. IUSON is looking for applicants who will contribute to our commitment to engage the city, state, nation, and beyond through research, teaching, and service, inconsideration of the rich diversity of our campus and community. Faculty are expected to maintain an active and productive external funding portfolio by pursuing external grant funding at a cadence set by the Executive Associate Dean for Research. Evidence includes submission and acquisition of primarily extramural funding, service as PI, co-PI, or key personnel on such funded projects, development of collaborative funding initiatives, and strategic use of pilot funding to support future external grant competitiveness. A pattern of ongoing grant activity, proposal development, and funding success is expected as part of a sustained program of scholarship and research excellence. Overall, the candidate’s responsibilities include contributing to IUSON’s research, teaching, and service missions. 1. The candidate is expected to develop a record of nationally and/or internationally disseminated and peer reviewed scholarship. 2. The candidate is expected to engage in high-quality teaching and mentoring. 3. The candidate is expected to actively participate in service at the school, campus, community, and professional levels. Basic Qualifications PhD is required. While a PhD in Nursing Science is preferred, we welcome candidates who have earned a PhD in a related field (e.g. public health, health sciences, psychology, sociology). Department Contact for Questions Tammy Leone at tkleone@iu.edu or 317-274-4157. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Open positions are available for August 2027 start dates. Applications received by December 1, 2026, will be given priority. The documents below must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34815 . Letter of application/Cover Letter (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Statement of Research and Teaching/Personal Statement (5-page limit) outlining achievements and goals in teaching, research, and service e.g. https://academicaffairs.indianapolis.iu.edu/Faculty-Affairs/PromotionTenure/guidelines-and-standards Contact list with three references For additional information, please contact Dr. Wendy Trueblood Miller, Acting Dean, Executive Associate Dean for Research at wrtruebl@iu.edu or Dr. Julie Otte, Executive Associate Dean of Analytics at jlelam@iu.edu. Additional information is available at nursing.iu.edu , including details about our Areas of Research Excellence: https://nursing.iu.edu/research/areas-research-excellence/index.html For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101759-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement: Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement: IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34845": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant / Associate Professor Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant / Associate Professor Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To apply, please submit an online application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com . For priority consideration, please apply by January 4, 2027. Your curriculum vitae and a letter of application are required. Names and contact information of three professional references are required on the application (referees will be requested to submit reference letters on letterhead with their signature). Transcripts will be required of individuals invited for an interview. The position will remain posted until filled. For questions about the application process, please contact Atlantis Nance at acnance@iu.edu Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant / Associate Professor Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Social Work Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ) anticipates hiring two tenure-track or tenured faculty positions at the assistant or associate rank at the Indianapolis campus. All areas of scholarly interest will be considered. Applicant experience should reflect potential for or an established record of success in scholarship, funded research and teaching. These are 10-month positions starting August 1, 2027. The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ), founded in 1911, is a university-wide system school with CSWE -accredited MSW and BSW Programs since 1923 and 1975 respectively, reaffirmed in 2021. School headquarters are located at Indiana University Indianapolis ( IUI ) with programs on eight IU campuses. The School is a founding member of the University’s health sciences Interprofessional Practice and Education Center, creating opportunities for students and faculty. IU provides faculty opportunities for competitive funding to support timely and innovative research, such as the Racial Justice Research Fund for which five of our faculty received awards. The School’s annual enrollment totals approximately 1,800 students across programs (PhD, MSW , BSW ) and campuses. With current leadership under Dean Kalea Benner, the School encompasses eight campuses plus an online MSW program and a Center for Social Health and Well-Being. IU Indianapolis Campus: IU Indianapolis is Indiana’s premier urban research university and academic health sciences campus, home to the largest medical school in the United States. Located in the center of the city, IU Indianapolis is an integral part of the Indianapolis metropolitan area of 1.9 million residents, serving 30,000+ students. Our location in the state capital facilitates work with government, business, and community organizations and enhances collaboration on research, education, economic development, and cultural initiatives. Boasting a desirable quality of life and reasonable cost of living, Indianapolis is situated in a dynamic regional economy and home to a variety of notable cultural. Basic Qualifications Qualifications include a doctoral-level degree in social work with a master’s degree in social work and two years of relevant post-master’s experience preferred. Candidates at the associate rank must meet established tenure standards for faculty in the IUSSW . Successful candidates will teach undergraduate and graduate courses, establish a funded research program resulting in sustained scholarship, and engage in service activities. Department Contact for Questions Atlantis Nance acnance@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Special Instructions To apply, please submit an online application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com . For priority consideration, please apply by January 4, 2027. Your curriculum vitae and a letter of application are required. Names and contact information of three professional references are required on the application (referees will be requested to submit reference letters on letterhead with their signature). Transcripts will be required of individuals invited for an interview. The position will remain posted until filled. For questions about the application process, please contact Atlantis Nance at acnance@iu.edu For Best Consideration Date 01/04/2027 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101757-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34843": {
      "title": "Mid-Career or Senior Tenure Track Faculty Professor, Indianapolis Campus",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Mid-Career or Senior Tenure Track Faculty Professor, Indianapolis Campus Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Mid-Career or Senior Tenure Track Faculty Professor, Indianapolis Campus Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Open positions are available for August 1, 2027 start dates. Leadership opportunities aligned with faculty expertise may be available. Applications received by December 1, 2026, will be given priority. The following documents must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34828 . Letter of application/Cover Letter (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Statement of Research and Teaching/Personal Statement (5-page limit) outlining achievements and goals in teaching, research, and service e.g. https://academicaffairs.indianapolis.iu.edu/Faculty-Affairs/PromotionTenure/guidelines-and-standards Contact list with six references For additional information, please contact Dr. Wendy Trueblood Miller, Acting Dean, Executive Associate Dean for Research at wrtruebl@iu.edu or Dr. Julie Otte, Executive Associate Dean of Analytics at jlelam@iu.edu. Additional information is available at nursing.iu.edu , including details about our Areas of Research Excellence: https://nursing.iu.edu/research/areas-research-excellence/index.html Posting Details Position Details Title Mid-Career or Senior Tenure Track Faculty Professor, Indianapolis Campus Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Nursing Location Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Nursing ( IUSON ) seeks qualified applicants for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of associate or full professor. We invite applicants who have a record of nationally and/or internationally disseminated and peer reviewed scholarship and emerging or sustained national reputation. IUSON offers a full range of academic programs from BSN to DNP and PhD. IUSON is looking for applicants who will contribute to our commitment to engage the city, state, nation, and beyond through research, teaching, and service, in consideration of our campus and community. Overall, the candidate’s responsibilities include contributing to IUSON’s research, teaching, and service missions. 1. The candidate is expected to have a record of nationally and/or internationally disseminated and peer reviewed scholarship. For appointment to Associate Professor, applicants must show evidence of an emerging national reputation. For appointment to Full Professor, applicants must show evidence of a sustained national reputation as demonstrated by a well-established and cumulative body of work in rank. The candidate must demonstrate an outstanding scholarly record of research, exhibited by high-impact peer-reviewed publications and a forward-looking, vigorous research agenda that has been continually securing competitive, external funding. The successful candidate will have the ability to work with interdisciplinary teams of interprofessional researchers across clinical and academic settings. 2. The candidate is expected to engage in high-quality teaching and mentoring. 3. The candidate is expected to actively participate in service at the school, campus, community, and professional levels. 4. The candidate is expected to engage in mentoring and grant proposal collaboration with more junior IUSON faculty. Basic Qualifications PhD is required. While a PhD in Nursing Science is preferred, candidates who have earned a PhD in a related field (e.g. public health, health sciences, psychology, sociology) will be considered. Candidates who hold a PhD in Nursing must hold or be eligible for an Indiana RN license. A successful history of external funding (e.g., NIH , HRSA , NLN , PCORI ) is strongly preferred. Appointment to academic rank and salary are dependent on evidence of scholarly, teaching and service achievements. Department Contact for Questions Tammy Leone at tkleone@iu.edu or 317-274-4157 Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Open positions are available for August 1, 2027 start dates. Leadership opportunities aligned with faculty expertise may be available. Applications received by December 1, 2026, will be given priority. The following documents must be submitted on-line to: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34828 . Letter of application/Cover Letter (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Statement of Research and Teaching/Personal Statement (5-page limit) outlining achievements and goals in teaching, research, and service e.g. https://academicaffairs.indianapolis.iu.edu/Faculty-Affairs/PromotionTenure/guidelines-and-standards Contact list with six references For additional information, please contact Dr. Wendy Trueblood Miller, Acting Dean, Executive Associate Dean for Research at wrtruebl@iu.edu or Dr. Julie Otte, Executive Associate Dean of Analytics at jlelam@iu.edu. Additional information is available at nursing.iu.edu , including details about our Areas of Research Excellence: https://nursing.iu.edu/research/areas-research-excellence/index.html For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101760-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement: Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement: IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34842": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Ophthalmology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Ophthalmology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Ophthalmology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Ophthalmology Specific Title Appointment Type Tenure Track Faculty Department IUSM - Ophthalmology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Eugene and Marilyn Glick Eye Institute at the Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) invites applications for an open-rank, tenure-track research faculty position in the Department of Ophthalmology. Candidates are expected to have a strong record of peer-reviewed publications and funding commensurate with their rank. We are keen to expand our research strength in real-world data and artificial intelligence (AI), new approach methods, and chronic disease prevention, with a focus on eye diseases and the cellular and molecular biology of vision. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and maintain an energetic, independent, and well-funded research program, and contribute to the educational, service, and/or clinical missions of the medical school. The Eugene and Marilyn Glick Eye Institute (https://medicine.iu.edu/ophthalmology), home to 40 full-time faculty, offers a highly collaborative and expanding research environment with state-of-the-art laboratory spaces, animal facilities, clinics, and faculty offices. Our faculty research focuses on major areas of vision science, including glaucoma, photoreceptor biology and retinal degeneration, diabetic retinopathy, retinal vascular and inflammatory diseases, optic neuropathy, and stem cell research. Our research group integrates molecular and cellular approaches, animal models, advanced ocular imaging, and stem cell models. The Department of Ophthalmology receives support from an unrestricted grant from Research to Prevent Blindness ( RPB ) and a T35 training grant for short-term research projects. Our faculty work alongside a vibrant research community across IUSM with complementary expertise in neuroscience, stem cell biology, traumatic brain injury, diabetic and metabolic diseases, medical genomics, bioinformatics, and AI. IUSM’s resources in advanced imaging, electrophysiology, behavioral phenotyping, microscopy, flow cytometry, biomarker and spatial molecular profiling, genomics, and neurochemistry provide exceptional opportunities for new faculty to build interdisciplinary programs. Faculty in the Department of Ophthalmology are active members of the Indiana University Interdisciplinary Biomedical and Biological Sciences ( IBMG ) graduate umbrella program, mentoring and training PhD students in a collaborative, interdisciplinary research environment. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter summarizing their relevant qualifications, research plan, a copy of their curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references. Basic Qualifications Qualified candidates will hold a Ph.D., M.D., or M.D./Ph.D., faculty rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Department Contact for Questions Inquiries about the position may be sent to Dr. John Lind, Professor and Chair, Department of Ophthalmology, jlind@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02526-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34835": {
      "title": "University Archivist",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | University Archivist Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> University Archivist Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title University Archivist Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis University Library Location Indianapolis Position Summary IU Indianapolis University Library seeks a University Archivist to join the Ruth Lilly Special Collections and Archives, part of Distinctive Collections. The University Archivist reports to the Director of Distinctive Collections and works collaboratively with library colleagues, faculty, and Indiana University partners to collect and provide access to university records. The position advances institutional priorities related to student and faculty research as well as community engagement and collaboration. This position will work with records of all types, including born-digital records such as websites, emails, and digital files, as well as audio-visual material. The University Archivist will support the full lifecycle of university records, which includes liaising with staff and faculty units about records management policies and the value of institutional records before transfer to the archives, as well as providing education and reference to researchers using these records of permanent value. Responsibilities Actively collect university records deemed of permanent value, including born digital materials and university websites Supervise and perform University Archives collection management activities: accessioning, processing, preservation, and creation of finding aids, including training and supervising student workers to assist in this work Provide reference services for University Archives, assisting virtual and in-person researchers (faculty, students, staff, and other researchers) in using university records Liaise with campus offices on the interpretation and implementation of records retention schedules Manage web archiving of university websites, including appraising URLs and setting appropriate capture schedules Promote interest in the history of IUI / IUPUI and the historic neighborhoods predating the campus, through reference, instruction, and outreach efforts Collaborate with Ruth Lilly Special Collections & Archives department members to improve service, provide access to collections, develop policies and procedures, and move department initiatives forward Secondary responsibilities Participate in development of records management policies in partnership with other campus archivists and the University Records Manager Serve as a member of the Digital Collections Advisory Group, which is charged with setting priorities, establishing best practices for digital collections creation, and evaluating potential digital collections projects Participate in University Library planning and decision-making activities Participate in University Library and campus faculty organizations Represent the library in local, state, and national library venues as appropriate. Participate in library and university initiatives that advance campus and community engagement with distinctive collections Support the mission, vision, values, and strategic priorities of University Library. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications Master’s degree from an ALA -accredited Library/Information Science program or an equivalent degree Minimum 2 years of professional experience in archives, including internships and part-time employment Proven success in archives, including knowledge and experience in archival best practices, principles, and current trends, including born digital records Experience processing large or complex archival collections and creating access tools (finding aids) for these collections Excellent communication, collaboration, and project management skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively with a team environment Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of records management Knowledge of web archiving Experience with digital collections, including digital formats and digitization procedures Knowledge of EAD (Encoded Archival Description) Department Contact for Questions Shauna Winter, HR Business Partner, shwint@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101641-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34834": {
      "title": "Research Data Librarian",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Data Librarian Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Data Librarian Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Data Librarian Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis University Library Location Indianapolis Position Summary IU Indianapolis University Library seeks a Research Data Librarian to join the Center for Digital Scholarship. Reporting to the Director of the Center for Digital Scholarship, this position collaborates with library colleagues, faculty, and Indiana University partners to support research data management, sharing, discovery, and responsible use across the research lifecycle. The librarian advances institutional research priorities related to open scholarship, compliance, and research impact services. The position contributes to the research information management work of the Center and the strategic priorities of the University Library. Responsibilities Provide consultations on research data management, including data management plans, metadata, storage, sharing, preservation, and repository selection. Support researchers in meeting funder, publisher, and institutional requirements for data sharing and public access. Assist researchers with qualitative and quantitative data discovery and use. Contribute to the development and promotion of research data services, repositories, and data discovery systems. Assist with the ingestion, curation, and management of datasets within an institutional repository. Design and deliver workshops on research data management, data sharing, discovery, and research metrics. Develop and implement outreach strategies for data and research information services. Promote the responsible use of research data, including AI-related considerations. Support faculty and administrators in interpreting research impact data for reporting and planning. Collaborate with liaison librarians and campus partners to integrate research impact and data services into faculty support programs. Advance open scholarship and responsible research practices through participation in library and university initiatives. About Indiana University Indianapolis and University Library IU Indianapolis is an urban-serving institution with a mission and a vision focused on both diversity and community engagement. University Library is a Power of Libraries award winner and home to numerous award-winning professionals. University Library is looking for librarians who will support our commitment to engage with the wider educational community and whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts are inclusive of the rich diversity of our campus and our city. Compensation and Application Minimum salary is $58,000, with additional funding for professional development and travel. Benefits include 22 vacation days, 10 paid holidays, health/dental insurance options, and retirement contributions of 10% to a Fidelity Investments retirement plan. A start-up package includes $10,000 for moving and ongoing professional development expenses. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications Master’s degree from an ALA -accredited Library and Information Science program or equivalent graduate degree. Interest in research data management, sharing, and reuse. Experience or interest in supporting faculty and student researchers. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Ability to adapt to evolving research practices and technologies. Strong library reference interview and database search skills. Knowledge of the higher education research environment, preferably in the social sciences, sciences, or biomedical fields. Preferred Qualifications Project management and cross-functional collaboration experience. Experience supporting a research team or lab with qualitative and/or quantitative research methods. Experience with FAIR , CARE , and best practices in data curation. Experience with research data repositories, metadata, or data discovery systems. Familiarity with reproducible research workflows and scholarly communication issues. Department Contact for Questions Shauna Winter, HR Business Partner shwint@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 08/10/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101609-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34832": {
      "title": "Assistant Clinical Instructor - Bloomington",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Clinical Instructor - Bloomington Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Clinical Instructor - Bloomington Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To apply, please submit an online application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com . For priority consideration, please apply by November 4, 2026 Your curriculum vitae and a letter of application are required. Names and contact information of three professional references are required on the application (referees will be requested to submit reference letters on letterhead with their signature). Transcripts will be required of individuals invited for an interview. The position will remain posted until filled. For questions about the application process, please contact Atlantis Nance acnance@iu.edu Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Clinical Instructor - Bloomington Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis School of Social Work Location Bloomington Position Summary Position Summary | The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ) invites applications for a non-tenure track faculty position (full-time clinical instructor) on the IU Bloomington campus. This twelve-month appointment, beginning January 1, 2027, will help facilitate the Wellness Partnership Initiative at IU Bloomington, providing supervision and oversight to students while serving as a liaison to partner schools. This position also carries expectations related to service on School and University committees. This appointment is renewable and contingent on student demand and available financial resources. The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ), founded in 1911, is a university-wide system school with CSWE -accredited MSW and BSW Programs since 1923 and 1975 respectively, reaffirmed in 2021. School headquarters are located at Indiana University – Indianapolis ( IUI ) with programs on seven IU campuses plus an online MSW . The School is a founding member of the University’s health sciences Interprofessional Practice and Education Center, creating opportunities for students and faculty. The School’s annual enrollment totals approximately 1,900 students across programs (PhD, MSW , BSW ) and campuses. The IU School of Social Work is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values that inform academic excellence. BoldItalicStrikethroughLinkHeadingQuoteCodeBulletsNumbersDecrease LevelIncrease LevelUndoRedo Position Summary | The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ) invites applications for a non-tenure track faculty position (full-time clinical instructor) on the IU Bloomington campus. This twelve-month appointment, beginning January 1, 2027, will help facilitate the Wellness Partnership Initiative at IU Bloomington, providing supervision and oversight to students while serving as a liaison to partner schools. This position also carries expectations related to service on School and University committees. This appointment is renewable and contingent on student demand and available financial resources. The Indiana University School of Social Work ( IUSSW ), founded in 1911, is a university-wide system school with CSWE -accredited MSW and BSW Programs since 1923 and 1975 respectively, reaffirmed in 2021. School headquarters are located at Indiana University – Indianapolis ( IUI ) with programs on seven IU campuses plus an online MSW . The School is a founding member of the University’s health sciences Interprofessional Practice and Education Center, creating opportunities for students and faculty. The School’s annual enrollment totals approximately 1,900 students across programs (PhD, MSW , BSW ) and campuses. The IU School of Social Work is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values that inform academic excellence. Basic Qualifications Essential qualifications for the position include an MSW degree from a CSWE -accredited program and two or more years of post- MSW practice experience. Preference will be given to applicants who demonstrate teaching experience at the BSW and/or MSW level and have practice experience in the areas of mental health, substance use disorders, and/or integrated behavioral health. Previous online or hybrid teaching experience is also preferred. Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience Department Contact for Questions Atlantis Nance acnance@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions To apply, please submit an online application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com . For priority consideration, please apply by November 4, 2026 Your curriculum vitae and a letter of application are required. Names and contact information of three professional references are required on the application (referees will be requested to submit reference letters on letterhead with their signature). Transcripts will be required of individuals invited for an interview. The position will remain posted until filled. For questions about the application process, please contact Atlantis Nance acnance@iu.edu For Best Consideration Date 11/04/2026 Expected Start Date 12/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101756-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34821": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant or Associate Professor of Kinesiology Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Indianapolis Department of Kinesiology – School of Health & Human Sciences Location Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Kinesiology in the School of Health & Human Sciences ( SHHS ) at Indiana University Indianapolis invites applications for a full-time, 10-month, tenure track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. The successful applicant will have the ability to teach and mentor both graduate (MS and PhD) and undergraduate (BS) students, will be responsible for developing an active and sustainable research agenda in their area of expertise, generate external funding, engage in teaching, and provide service to the school, university, community, and profession. Individuals with translational research and/or community engagement experiences are especially encouraged to apply. The Department of Kinesiology offers a BS with plans of study in: - Exercise Science - Applied Fitness and Sports Performance - Physical Education Teacher Education - Nutrition and Wellness SHHS also offers PhD and MS degrees in Human Performance and Rehabilitation Sciences. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 1, 2026, and will be accepted until the position is filled. The target starting date is August 1, 2027. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their letter of application, curriculum vitae, statements of research and teaching, and the names and contact information for three references. The letter of intent should include a summary of qualifications and experience, key motivation for application, and alignment with IU Indianapolis and SHHS , overall. IU Indianapolis is Indiana’s premier urban public research university and holds Community Engagement and R1 research Carnegie Classifications. It is home to more than 22,000 students pursuing degrees across 15 distinct schools, including the Indiana University School of Medicine and the Indiana Center for Musculoskeletal Health ( ICMH ). IU Indianapolis has earned national recognition for its innovative health and life sciences initiatives, providing extensive opportunities for collaborative, interdisciplinary research. The university is making significant investments in research, including the launch of two new institutes: the Convergent Bioscience and Technology Institute ( CBATI ) and the Institute for Human Health and Wellbeing (H2W). Additionally, a $138 million grant from the Lilly Endowment will support the IU LAB , a state-of-the-art facility at 16 Tech site designed to position Indiana as a national leader in bioscience discovery, commercialization, and talent development. SHHS is looking for scholars who will contribute to our commitment to engage the city, state, nation, and beyond through research, teaching, and service. Potential scholars should take into consideration and value the attributes of our campus and city populations as we prepare our graduates to enter and lead in an increasingly complex world. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . Individuals who require a reasonable accommodation in order to participate in the search process must notify the School’s HR Business Partner, Myrna Palacios, at mypala@iu.edu , in advance. Basic Qualifications Ph.D. in Exercise Science, Kinesiology, Human Movement Science, or closely related field. Demonstrated teaching and/or professional experience in: Sport and Human Performance Testing and Interpretation. Exercise Leadership and Program Design. Behavioral Aspects of Physical Activity and Exercise. Active research agenda aligned with the discipline of kinesiology. Demonstrated potential or history of securing independent external research funding. Experience in mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and/or professional students. Department Contact for Questions For more information on the position, please contact department chair Dr. Mark Urtel at murtel1@iu.edu. Additional information on the School of Health & Human Sciences, as well as the Department of Kinesiology is available at: https://shhs.indianapolis.iu.edu/index.html . Additional Qualifications Post-doctoral training or equivalent translational field/industry experience. Active interest or demonstrated success in interdisciplinary research collaborations across SHHS , the IU School of Medicine, or campus research institutes (e.g., ICMH , CBATI , H2W). A demonstrated ability to communicate and collaborate effectively with students, employees, and other members of the university community. Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101716-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34933": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Dermatology and Medical and Molecular Genetics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Associate Professor of Dermatology and Medical and Molecular Genetics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Associate Professor of Dermatology and Medical and Molecular Genetics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please provide a CV and contact information for 3 references. Posting Details Position Details Title Associate Professor of Dermatology and Medical and Molecular Genetics Specific Title Appointment Type Tenure Track Faculty Department IUSM - Dermatology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Dermatology along with the Department of Medicine, Medical and Molecular Genetics at Indiana University School of Medicine, invites applications for a tenure track faculty position. We seek outstanding investigators conducting innovative basic, translational, computational, or clinical research in areas related to dermatology, genetics, genomics, immunology, cancer biology, precision medicine, wound healing, bioinformatics, or other biomedical disciplines that complement and expand existing departmental strengths. Applicants should have excellent research credentials and training, including high impact publications. The IUI campus is located near downtown Indianapolis, a city that uniquely combines the cultural amenities of urban life with the residential qualities of smaller communities and is highly affordable. As the major center for industry and government within the state, Indianapolis has a wide variety of research and training sites. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications Doctorate or Terminal degree is required. Candidates should have Ph.D., MD., or Ph.D./M.D. The faculty rank for this tenure-track position is open and will be determined based on the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. Department Contact for Questions Inquiries about this position should be sent to Nikki Wilson, wilsonnl@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Applicants must demonstrate the ability to initiate a research program that can attract external funding to sustain it. Special Instructions Please provide a CV and contact information for 3 references. Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IUSM-02558-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? (Open Ended Question) * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Letter of Application Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34929": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Research Professor in Medicine - Hematology/Oncology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate Research Professor in Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate Research Professor in Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate Research Professor in Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Specific Title Appointment Type Scientist Track Faculty Department IUSM - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Indiana University School of Medicine and the IU Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center seek a highly motivated Research Assistant Professor to join the HEAL Laboratory. The successful candidate will contribute to a dynamic translational research program focused on the mechanisms of head and neck cancer progression, immunotherapy response and resistance, tumor immunology, and the development of novel therapeutic strategies. This non-tenure track faculty position provides an opportunity to conduct innovative laboratory-based and translational research within a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment. The successful applicant will be expected to develop an independent area of investigation that complements ongoing laboratory efforts while contributing to collaborative projects and external grant funding initiatives. Responsibilities Lead and conduct translational and laboratory-based research focused on head and neck squamous cell carcinoma and related malignancies. Investigate mechanisms of immune evasion, treatment resistance, and therapeutic response in preclinical models. Design, execute, analyze, and publish high-impact research studies. Develop and maintain patient-derived and primary cell culture models. Perform and oversee immunologic and cellular assays, including immune cell functional analyses and cytotoxicity assays. Design and conduct in vivo studies using murine tumor models in compliance with institutional animal care regulations. Utilize advanced flow cytometry approaches for immune profiling and mechanistic studies. Collaborate with clinical and basic science investigators across Indiana University and the IU Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center. Mentor graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, research staff, and trainees. Prepare and submit grant applications, including federal, foundation, and industry-sponsored funding opportunities. Contribute to manuscript preparation, laboratory management, and scientific presentations at national and international meetings. Expectations for Success The ideal candidate will be an innovative and collaborative scientist with a strong interest in translating laboratory discoveries into improved treatments for patients with head and neck cancers. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to developing externally funded research programs and be prepared to lead grant-writing efforts within the first year of appointment. The successful applicant will be expected to contribute substantially to the growth of the HEAL Lab by generating novel scientific insights, fostering collaborations, and helping secure sustainable research funding. This position is intended for an individual seeking to establish an independent, extramurally funded research program. The successful candidate will be expected to pursue career development and independent funding mechanisms, including NIH K-series awards, R21, R03, and R01 grants, Department of Defense funding opportunities, American Cancer Society grants, and other foundation-supported research awards, with the goal of achieving long-term research independence and national recognition in the field of cancer biology and immunotherapy. Academic Environment The HEAL Laboratory is embedded within the Indiana University School of Medicine and the IU Melvin and Bren Simon Comprehensive Cancer Center, providing access to outstanding core facilities, including flow cytometry, genomics, biostatistics, animal modeling, and translational research resources. The laboratory maintains strong collaborations among clinicians, translational investigators, and basic scientists with a shared mission of improving outcomes for patients with head and neck cancers. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of health sciences companies. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city and surrounding suburbs enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport that has been named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation . The search committee will begin evaluating applications as they are received, and applications will continue to be reviewed until this position is filled. Basic Qualifications Required PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Immunology, Cancer Biology, Molecular Biology, Cell Biology, Experimental Therapeutics, or a related discipline. Postdoctoral experience with evidence of scientific productivity. Strong publication record in peer-reviewed journals. Experience with mammalian cell culture techniques, including primary cell cultures. Experience with immunologic assays and cellular functional studies. Experience with flow cytometry data acquisition and analysis. Experience with animal models of cancer. Demonstrated ability to independently design experiments, analyze data, and communicate scientific findings. Preferred Research experience in head and neck cancer, solid tumor immunology, or cancer therapeutics. Expertise in immunotherapy, tumor microenvironment biology, or mechanisms of therapy resistance. Experience with multi-parameter and/or spectral flow cytometry, immune phenotyping, and functional immune assays. Experience performing cytotoxicity assays involving T-cell, NK-cell, or other immune effector cell populations. Knowledge of molecular biology techniques such as transgenic expression/repression, DNA vector construction and cloning, qPCR, Western blotting, CRISPR -based approaches, next-generation sequencing, or spatial/transcriptomic analyses. Prior participation in scientific meeting (poster, oral presentation) Prior participation in grant writing and extramural funding applications. · Evidence of developing an independent research program. Demonstrated commitment to pursuing independent research funding and building a sustainable translational research program is highly desirable. Department Contact for Questions Sabrina Hetzel (HR contact) at snwall@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02553-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Application Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34908": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic &amp; Imaging Sciences(10 month)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic &amp; Imaging Sciences(10 month) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences(10 month) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences(10 month) Specific Title Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Radiology & Imaging Sciences Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs Radiography Program Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences(10 month) Primary Responsibilities The primary responsibilities of radiography faculty member in the Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs of the Indiana University School of Medicine, Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences are teaching and service. Teaching may involve classroom or laboratory instruction at the undergraduate level. Service is rendered through administrative and committee responsibilities at the Program, School and University levels, and through community and professional activities. Additionally, faculty must demonstrate a commitment to lifelong learning by participating in continuing education activities, pursuing advanced degrees and certifications, and engaging in clinical practice experiences that ensure continued professional growth and the maintenance of current knowledge and skills. Specific faculty responsibilities are assigned by the Director of the Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs but are not limited to, the following: · Teaching in those areas to which the faculty member is most suited by education and experience · Counseling of professional and pre-professional students · Assisting the Director in the administration of the program through contributions during faculty meetings, service on program committees, and the completion of assigned projects · Contributing to the University through service on School and University committees · Participating in sufficient continuing learning experiences and/or patient care interactions to remain competent in the field · Representing Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs by providing accurate and appropriate communication to all constituents of the Programs, School and University · Participating in Program and School level continuing education activities · Submitting annual goals related to teaching and service In addition, the faculty member will have the following administrative responsibilities: · Planning and directing classroom activities · Developing syllabi for all courses assigned · Developing the methods used for evaluating students and measuring academic progress · Assisting in accreditation documentation, assessment, and submission IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Ready to apply? Submit your letter of interest and current CV ( PDF ) via the IU application portal: Basic Qualifications Qualifications · Current or previous teaching experience in a radiography program · Meets qualifications required by the Joint Review Committee on Education in Radiologic Technology ( JRCERT ) to serve as a didactic faculty member. These qualifications include: o Holds, at a minimum, a baccalaureate degree , master degree preferred o Individuals with a bachelor’s degree will be appointed as Acting Lecturer until master’s degree has been completed. Upon master’s degree completion, the appointment will be updated to Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences o Is proficient in curriculum development, supervision, instruction, evaluation, and academic advising o Documents two years clinical experience in the professional discipline o Documents a minimum of one year of experience as an instructor in a JRECRT accredited program o Holds a current credential with the American Registry of Radiologic Technologists, in radiography. Department Contact for Questions Carla Ewing Associate Director, HR, IUSM Radiology & Imaging Sciences carewing@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02557-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34907": {
      "title": "Nuclear Medicine Clinical Coordinator &amp; Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic &amp; Imaging Sciences",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Nuclear Medicine Clinical Coordinator &amp; Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic &amp; Imaging Sciences Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Nuclear Medicine Clinical Coordinator & Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Nuclear Medicine Clinical Coordinator & Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences Specific Title Appointment Type Clinical Track Faculty Department IUSM - Radiology & Imaging Sciences Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary Indiana University School of Medicine Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs Nuclear Medicine Program Nuclear Medicine Clinical Coordinator Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences Primary Responsibilities The primary responsibilities of the full-time Nuclear Medicine Program Clinical Coordinator in the Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs of the Indiana University School of Medicine, Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences are teaching and service. Teaching may involve classroom, laboratory, or clinical courses at the undergraduate level. Service is rendered through administrative and committee responsibilities at the Program, School and University levels, and through community and professional activities. Additionally, faculty must demonstrate a commitment to lifelong learning by participating in continuing education activities, pursuing advanced degrees and certifications, and engaging in clinical practice experiences that ensure continued professional growth and the maintenance of current knowledge and skills. Specific faculty responsibilities are assigned by the Director of the Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs and include, but are not limited to, the following: · Teaching in those areas to which the faculty member is most suited by education and experience · Counseling of professional and pre-professional students · Assisting the Director in the administration of the program through contributions during faculty meetings, service on program committees, and the completion of assigned projects · Contributing to the University through service on School and University committees · Participating in sufficient continuing learning experiences and/or patient care interactions to remain competent in the field · Representing Radiologic and Imaging Sciences Programs by providing accurate and appropriate communication to all constituents of the Programs, School and University · Participating in Program and School level continuing education activities · Submitting annual goals related to teaching and service In addition, the Nuclear Medicine Program Clinical Coordinator has the following administrative responsibilities: · Planning and directing the daily operations related to delivery of all clinical experience activities · Developing a common syllabus for all clinical experience courses · Administering and updating the Clinical Competency Records Database. · Developing and publishing all clinical experience schedules, in consultation with the NMT director and the clinical faculty · Developing the methods used for evaluating students and measuring clinical progress · Developing the methods used for students to evaluate each clinical site, course, faculty member, etc. · Developing clinical activities that compliment/extend the nuclear medicine clinical experience (e.g., 1st year correlation activities; venipuncture, etc.) · Organizing and assigning tasks to clinical faculty for labs conducted as part of the clinical experience · Serving as first-line leader for the clinical faculty, to assist in solving student issues related to performance or behavior in the clinical experience courses · Recommending changes for policies and procedures used for clinical courses · Assisting the nuclear medicine program director in reviewing effectiveness of existing clinical education sites and developing new clinical sites as needed · Assisting in accreditation documentation, assessment, and submission IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Ready to apply? Submit your letter of interest and current CV ( PDF ) via the IU application portal: Basic Qualifications Qualifications · Current or previous teaching experience in a nuclear medicine program · Meets qualifications required by the Joint Review Committee on Education in Nuclear Medicine Technology ( JRCNMT ) to serve as a nuclear medicine Clinical Coordinator. These qualifications include: o Holds, at a minimum, a baccalaureate degree, Individuals with a bachelor’s degree will be appointed as Acting Lecturer until master’s degree has been completed. Upon master’s degree completion, the appointment will be updated to Assistant Professor of Clinical Radiologic & Imaging Sciences o Is proficient in curriculum development, supervision, instruction, evaluation, and academic advising o Documents two years clinical experience in the professional discipline o Documents a minimum of one year of experience as an instructor in a JRCNMT -accredited program o Holds American Registry of Radiologic Technologists current registration in Nuclear Medicine or NMTCB Department Contact for Questions Carla Ewing Associate Director, HR, IUSM Radiology & Imaging Sciences carewing@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date Posting Number IUSM-02556-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34901": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Pediatrics Specific Title Appointment Type Postdoctoral Fellow Department IUSM - Pediatrics Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The fellowship is situated within the Health Equity through Autism Research Translation ( HEART ) Lab, directed by Dr. Rebecca McNally Keehn. The HEART Lab focuses on reducing autism and neurodevelopmental health disparities through the development and evaluation of innovative models of care both within the US and globally. Our team of scientists, health care clinicians, caregivers of children with neurodevelopmental disabilities, autistic advocates and learners are passionate about applying cutting-edge research methods to solve real-world health problems and improve the lives of children, their families, and the health care clinicians and systems that care for them. Our areas of research focus include: understanding disparities in autism services to drive new methods for improving health care access and equity; building capacity of primary care to improve equitable access to autism evaluation in partnership with Indiana’s Early Autism Evaluation Hub system; cultural adaptation of autism evaluation tools for use in resource-constrained local and global communities; integration of clinical and biobehavioral approaches to advance early autism diagnosis. Fellows will build a primary focus in one or two of the areas below while gaining exposure to cutting edge translational and health services research methodologies. All areas of focus offer the opportunity to contribute to NIH -funded research studies. - Innovative primary care health service models. We partner with a statewide network of primary care practices working to lower the age of autism diagnosis. We have an ongoing multi-site clinical trial designed to optimize primary care clinician training in autism diagnosis as well as an international study of autism persistence. Fellows can build skills in implementation science and clinical trial methodology as well as quality improvement and mixed-methods approaches. -Application of eye-tracking biomarkers for clinical diagnosis. Our team developed and is testing a model in which trained primary care clinicians diagnose autism in toddlers, supported by eye-tracking biomarkers. Fellows can build skills in to biomarker development and validation, diagnostic accuracy, and clinical decision support. - Global autism health. Through partnership with AMPATH -Kenya, we study autism outcomes and neurobehavioral markers in young children born to mothers with HIV in Western Kenya. Fellows will build skills in cultural adaptation of diagnostic and caregiver-report measures, screening and diagnostic accuracy, eye-tracking in low-resource settings, and community-engaged research capacity building. Two appointment tracks are available: a 100% research track and a 50:50 clinical/research track. Research track (100% research). Suited to candidates in cognitive neuroscience, developmental psychology, global health, implementation science, data science, or related fields. No clinical activities are required. Fellows will be mentored by Dr. Brandon Keehn, Professor of Speech Language and Hearing Sciences and Psychological Sciences at Purdue University. Clinical/research track (50% clinical, 50% research). Suited to clinical/school psychologists seeking clinical licensure hours and research expertise. Clinical activities include developmental evaluations of toddlers in primary care research clinics, research-reliable ADOS -2 administration, and interdisciplinary diagnostic clinics serving toddlers through young adults. Fellows receive weekly supervision from a licensed psychologist and live supervision in clinic, supporting hours toward licensure. Research focus is flexible and dependent upon the candidate’s background and professional goals. Indiana University is a multi-campus public research institution, and a world leader in professional, medical, and technological education with an annual operating budget of $2.1 billion. IUSM is the largest medical school in the United States with a student body that includes over 43% women and 36% ethnic minorities. IUSM scientists and physicians were awarded over $217million in research funding from the National Institutes of Health ( NIH ) in 2021, setting a school record for research funding for the sixth straight year. Additionally, IUSM provides an interdisciplinary and interactive scientific environment with many multidisciplinary centers and state-of-the-art core facilities. Indianapolis is a diverse, centrally located city that lies within 3 driving hours of other major population centers such as Chicago, Cincinnati, and Louisville. It is 14th largest city in the United States and is growing and thriving economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation, which contribute over $44 billion annually to the local economy. Our dynamic downtown is a bustling commercial center, entertainment destination and residential neighborhood. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues and outdoor recreation areas. For additional information on life in Indy, visit https://medicine.iu.edu/indianapolis. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. IUSM is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Basic Qualifications PhD or MD/PhD in cognitive neuroscience, developmental psychology, global health, implementation science, data science, or related fields Department Contact for Questions Rebecca McNally mcnallyr@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date 07/01/2027 Posting Number IUSM-02552-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34947": {
      "title": "Chair Department of Physics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Chair Department of Physics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Chair Department of Physics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Chair Department of Physics Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Indianapolis Department of Physics – School of Science Location Indianapolis Position Summary Chair, Department of Physics, School of Science, IU Indianapolis Internal applicants only The IU-Indy Physics Department invites applications for the position of Chair of the Department of Physics from tenured candidates, particularly those at the full Professor rank. The Chair of the Department of Physics in the School of Science (SoS) at IU Indianapolis is expected to provide visionary leadership for the unit, including developing and executing a clear vision to strengthen the Department. The Chair is expected to attract, mentor, and retain a cohort of the highest quality faculty, staff and students, and to develop and maintain programs at both the undergraduate and graduate levels that highlight its strengths. The Chair will: · Develop a vision and execute a plan for strategic growth of the academic and research enterprises that align with the strategic plans of the SoS and IU Indianapolis. · Lead a team of academically strong individuals and on issues including recruitment, retention, and management and development of faculty and staff. · Manage the departmental budget, keeping annual expenditure and income in balance. · Contribute to graduate and undergraduate teaching, and curriculum and program development by advancing and modernizing the curriculum. · Advance the Department’s research funding and reputation and support efforts to increase externally funded research. · Work collaboratively within the Department, the School of Science, and across IU Indianapolis. · ·Demonstrate talent in consensus building, strategic planning, vision, and innovation. · Demonstrate communication and interpersonal skills needed to be an effective leader. · Participate in the alumni relations and fundraising activities of the SoS. The successful candidate is expected to be an internationally recognized scholar in a sub-discipline of Physics, as evident from publication, citation, and funding records; possess a proven track record of service to the department and the SoS; have a successful record of teaching, research and creative activity, and service at IU Indianapolis; and have experience in working with multiple communities to broaden participation in science. Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae and a leadership statement. Review of applications will begin on Oct. 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Dr. Ruihua Cheng e-mail: rucheng@iu.edu Basic Qualifications Tenured Internal Physics Professor Department Contact for Questions Dr. Ruihua Cheng e-mail: rucheng@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Other – Leadership Statement third document to be submitted Salary and Rank Commensurate with rank and experience Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/15/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101718-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Other Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34940": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor of Medicine-Clinical Pharmacology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Research Professor of Medicine-Clinical Pharmacology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Research Professor of Medicine-Clinical Pharmacology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Research Professor of Medicine-Clinical Pharmacology Specific Title Appointment Type Scientist Track Faculty Department IUSM - Medicine - Clinical Pharmacology Campus IU School of Medicine Indianapolis Position Summary The Department of Medicine, Division of Clinical Pharmacology at Indiana University School of Medicine ( IUSM ) invites applications for a research-track Assistant Professor position. We are seeking a dynamic, collaborative investigator to expand our early-stage drug discovery and medicinal chemistry efforts targeting Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias ( ADRD ). The Division of Clinical Pharmacology conducts groundbreaking translational research in the area of drug discovery and development, drug interactions, and genomic variants that impact therapeutic effectiveness. The multi-disciplinary research includes collaborations through the Indiana University School of Medicine and the broader IU community. More information about our growing division can be found here. IU School of Medicine is committed to being a welcoming campus community and we seek candidates whose research, teaching, and community engagement efforts contribute to robust learning and working environments for all students, staff, and faculty. We invite individuals who will join us in our mission to improve health equity and well-being for all throughout the state of Indiana. Indianapolis is the capital and most populous city in the State of Indiana. It is growing economically thanks to a strong corporate base anchored by the life sciences. Indiana is home to one of the largest concentrations of health sciences companies in the nation. Indianapolis has a sophisticated blend of charm and culture with a wonderful balance of business and leisure. The growing residential base is supported by rich amenities and quality of life – the city possesses a variety of professional sports, arts venues, and outdoor recreation areas. Residents of this dynamic city, and surrounding suburbs, enjoy leading educational systems and top-ranked universities, paired with a diverse population. Indianapolis International Airport is a top-ranked international airport, being named “Best Airport in North America” by Airports Council International for many years. For additional information on life in Indy: https://faculty.medicine.iu.edu/relocation/ . Basic Qualifications Education : Doctorate: PhD (or equivalent) in chemistry, biochemistry, pharmacology, or a closely related field Experience : Minimum of 1-3 years postdoctoral experience is required Department Contact for Questions Cheryl Weatherholt at weatherc@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Key Responsibilities Research Program: Build and sustain an innovative research program focused on evaluating novel gene targets and prosecuting early-stage drug discovery projects for ADRD Collaboration: Work closely with interdisciplinary teams and researchers across IUSM to advance collaborative therapeutic pipelines Funding: Seek and secure independent funding within 1-3 years of the appointment Special Instructions Priority Application Review Deadline Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IUSM-02551-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Personal Contact: School of Medicine recruiter Personal Contact: IUHP Physician Recruiter Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Are you a dual career partner (your partner or spouse is already being recruited)? Yes No Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
